1
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:06,140
Hi, everyone

2
00:00:06,140 --> 00:00:07,500
Let's get started

3
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,040
In the last couple of lectures, we've

4
00:00:09,040 --> 00:00:12,720
been talking about training RL and imitation

5
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:16,120
learning systems that can do multiple different tasks

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:20,630
And we first looked at this from the lens of multi-task imitation

7
00:00:20,630 --> 00:00:22,000
and reinforcement learning, where

8
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,420
you condition a policy or a Q-function not just on the state

9
00:00:26,420 --> 00:00:29,320
but also on some sort of descriptor of the task

10
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:30,860
or where there are also scenarios

11
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:33,240
in the goal condition setting where that descriptor could

12
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:36,160
actually be a state itself, like a goal state

13
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,900
that you're trying to reach

14
00:00:37,900 --> 00:00:39,520
And then more recently, we were talking

15
00:00:39,520 --> 00:00:43,000
about meta-reinforcement learning, where you could think

16
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,080
of that instead of conditioning on a description of the task

17
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:48,160
you want to do-- you actually need to figure out

18
00:00:48,160 --> 00:00:51,600
from a few examples and a little bit of experience

19
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:54,240
with rewards, what the task is

20
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:58,440
And you're trying to train this few-shot policy that

21
00:00:58,440 --> 00:01:01,770
can adapt with a small amount of experience

22
00:01:01,770 --> 00:01:04,530
Now, today, we'll be kind of building

23
00:01:04,530 --> 00:01:08,530
on some of these concepts and trying to not just train

24
00:01:08,530 --> 00:01:11,730
a policy to do multiple tasks but to actually string together

25
00:01:11,730 --> 00:01:18,210
behaviors for multiple tasks into a longer horizon task

26
00:01:18,210 --> 00:01:21,690
So this is where we're going to be talking

27
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:24,450
about where hierarchy might be helpful for solving

28
00:01:24,450 --> 00:01:25,910
long-horizon tasks

29
00:01:25,910 --> 00:01:27,410
And then we'll be talking about some

30
00:01:27,410 --> 00:01:30,570
of the key design choices behind hierarchical imitation

31
00:01:30,570 --> 00:01:32,410
and reinforcement learning methods

32
00:01:32,410 --> 00:01:35,330
including how to represent skills and goals, when

33
00:01:35,330 --> 00:01:39,390
to move on from one goal or task to another

34
00:01:39,390 --> 00:01:41,850
and the supervision for the different levels

35
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:44,080
of the hierarchy

36
00:01:44,080 --> 00:01:46,210
And then lastly, we'll go through a couple examples

37
00:01:46,210 --> 00:01:48,850
of what these systems could actually look like because there

38
00:01:48,850 --> 00:01:50,650
are a variety of different ways that you

39
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:53,930
could design these sort of hierarchical systems

40
00:01:53,930 --> 00:01:56,450
So the task that we'll be talking about today

41
00:01:56,450 --> 00:01:58,990
are long-horizon tasks

42
00:01:58,990 --> 00:02:00,470
And by long-horizon tasks, I mean

43
00:02:00,470 --> 00:02:03,070
tasks that take a while to complete

44
00:02:03,070 --> 00:02:06,720
and might consist of a number of different subtasks

45
00:02:06,720 --> 00:02:08,350
or a number of different steps that you

46
00:02:08,350 --> 00:02:11,250
need to take in some sort of sequence or order

47
00:02:11,250 --> 00:02:13,870
in order to complete it successfully

48
00:02:13,870 --> 00:02:17,030
So this could be something like cooking a meal

49
00:02:17,030 --> 00:02:22,290
It could be for a robot system, for an LLM agent

50
00:02:22,290 --> 00:02:26,510
It could be trying to fix a bug that's causing a neural network

51
00:02:26,510 --> 00:02:28,350
training loss to explode

52
00:02:28,350 --> 00:02:31,550
For an autonomous vehicle, it could be driving to Yosemite

53
00:02:31,550 --> 00:02:35,430
For a different LLM, it could be giving feedback

54
00:02:35,430 --> 00:02:37,690
on a long report

55
00:02:37,690 --> 00:02:39,110
And for these tasks, you can think

56
00:02:39,110 --> 00:02:41,110
about how there are oftentimes different steps

57
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:42,390
that you need to complete

58
00:02:42,390 --> 00:02:46,730
Some of those steps might be interdependent on one another

59
00:02:46,730 --> 00:02:49,150
where it makes sense to do one before the other

60
00:02:49,150 --> 00:02:51,270
And other tasks might be something

61
00:02:51,270 --> 00:02:53,130
where they're less interdependent

62
00:02:53,130 --> 00:02:57,800
and it's actually OK to do those in parallel

63
00:02:57,800 --> 00:03:02,760
These are really hard problems for AI systems to solve

64
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:06,280
in general

65
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:07,860
There's a number of reasons for that

66
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:11,360
So the first is that when you are

67
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:13,400
doing a task that takes a long time

68
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:17,760
you're visiting a large number of states along the way

69
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:19,040
to get there

70
00:03:19,040 --> 00:03:21,140
And every new state that you visit

71
00:03:21,140 --> 00:03:24,160
you need to be able to make decisions for

72
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:27,320
So you basically just have a much broader distribution

73
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:31,280
that your policy needs to be successful on

74
00:03:31,280 --> 00:03:34,720
There's also a lot of different opportunities to make mistakes

75
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:36,600
And if you make a mistake, you need

76
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:38,520
to be able to recover from that mistake

77
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:40,740
if it's something that you can actually recover from

78
00:03:40,740 --> 00:03:42,200
And separate from mistakes, there's

79
00:03:42,200 --> 00:03:44,600
also just opportunities to get stuck

80
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:48,080
For example, if there's one kind of mistake

81
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:49,800
that might, for example, not lead

82
00:03:49,800 --> 00:03:52,320
you to actually make progress on the task

83
00:03:52,320 --> 00:03:55,220
And if you're not making progress

84
00:03:55,220 --> 00:03:58,580
then you'll actually just end up in the same state again

85
00:03:58,580 --> 00:04:01,580
And you'll keep on making that same mistake over and over again

86
00:04:01,580 --> 00:04:04,860
and essentially just get stuck at performing the task

87
00:04:04,860 --> 00:04:07,140
And these kinds of mistakes are particularly

88
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:11,160
gnarly because when you end up in the same state again

89
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:13,620
your policy will keep on deterministically staying

90
00:04:13,620 --> 00:04:15,340
in that state and never making forward

91
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:18,140
progress towards the goal

92
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:21,240
And then what's the main idea behind hierarchy

93
00:04:21,240 --> 00:04:27,020
So we've talked about multi-task policies, where you have some--

94
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:30,100
actually, let me draw this further to the right

95
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:36,860
We have some policy that takes as input some state

96
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:39,020
Maybe the state at some timestep

97
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:42,940
And it also takes as input some task or some goal

98
00:04:42,940 --> 00:04:44,600
I'm going to write this as g for now

99
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:46,940
because this task or goal could take

100
00:04:46,940 --> 00:04:49,060
a number of different forms

101
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:52,380
And this is passed into your policy

102
00:04:52,380 --> 00:04:56,470
to produce actions that are trying to address

103
00:04:56,470 --> 00:05:00,350
that particular goal

104
00:05:00,350 --> 00:05:02,870
The idea for trying to solve long-horizon tasks

105
00:05:02,870 --> 00:05:07,310
is if we have a policy that can do a number of different things

106
00:05:07,310 --> 00:05:10,790
Then we could try to break up a bigger

107
00:05:10,790 --> 00:05:16,070
task into different subtasks and then

108
00:05:16,070 --> 00:05:20,990
use one policy to actually tell this policy what task it should

109
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:25,550
be doing at any given point in time

110
00:05:25,550 --> 00:05:31,950
So for example, you could have another policy here

111
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:36,030
that's actually outputting the goals to be doing

112
00:05:36,030 --> 00:05:38,750
And this policy should also have information

113
00:05:38,750 --> 00:05:42,150
about the state as input so that it knows where it is

114
00:05:42,150 --> 00:05:48,190
and what goal makes sense for the agent to do

115
00:05:48,190 --> 00:05:52,990
And then optionally, this is often called a low-level policy

116
00:05:52,990 --> 00:05:55,990
or we'll use pi LL to refer

117
00:05:55,990 --> 00:05:57,390
This is the low-level policy

118
00:05:57,390 --> 00:05:59,070
This is doing shorter tasks

119
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:02,170
And we can refer to this as a high-level policy that's

120
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:06,450
outputting goals for the low-level policy to accomplish

121
00:06:06,450 --> 00:06:08,990
And this policy, it can have a task

122
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:09,490
Sorry

123
00:06:09,490 --> 00:06:10,590
I wrote this as low level

124
00:06:10,590 --> 00:06:14,330
This should be high level

125
00:06:14,330 --> 00:06:16,490
If you want your policy to be able to do

126
00:06:16,490 --> 00:06:19,890
multiple long-horizon tasks, then this high-level policy

127
00:06:19,890 --> 00:06:23,490
could also have some sort of task descriptor as input as

128
00:06:23,490 --> 00:06:25,490
well, or some sort of goal as input

129
00:06:25,490 --> 00:06:30,330
And so we, for example, could have goal high level as input

130
00:06:30,330 --> 00:06:33,090
to this policy, if you want to be completing

131
00:06:33,090 --> 00:06:35,990
not just one long-horizon task but multiple long-horizon tasks

132
00:06:35,990 --> 00:06:37,990
Like not just driving to Yosemite

133
00:06:37,990 --> 00:06:40,730
but also being able to drive to the Grand

134
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:46,770
Canyon or LA or Seattle or something like that

135
00:06:46,770 --> 00:06:50,570
And the kind of crux underlying this idea

136
00:06:50,570 --> 00:06:53,780
is that if we can break up a really long task into smaller

137
00:06:53,780 --> 00:07:00,460
parts, then we might be able to better address the problem

138
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:03,700
And say, for example, your long-horizon task

139
00:07:03,700 --> 00:07:05,340
is to bake a cheesecake

140
00:07:05,340 --> 00:07:06,920
This is a really, really hard problem

141
00:07:06,920 --> 00:07:10,620
And if you just tried to train a policy like a robot policy

142
00:07:10,620 --> 00:07:13,460
to output motor commands for doing this task

143
00:07:13,460 --> 00:07:15,780
it's really hard to figure out what you should be doing

144
00:07:15,780 --> 00:07:17,940
at any given point in time

145
00:07:17,940 --> 00:07:20,560
But if you're just starting out, you can think about, OK

146
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:22,440
what's the first step for accomplishing this

147
00:07:22,440 --> 00:07:23,860
Maybe you need to buy ingredients

148
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:25,900
Then what's the first step of buying ingredients

149
00:07:25,900 --> 00:07:27,560
Well, maybe you need to go to the store

150
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:28,900
And how do you get to the store

151
00:07:28,900 --> 00:07:30,660
Well, maybe you need to walk to the door

152
00:07:30,660 --> 00:07:33,360
To walk to the door, you might take a step

153
00:07:33,360 --> 00:07:34,320
How do you take a step

154
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:36,500
You might contract a muscle

155
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:39,180
And so you can basically break down a bigger task

156
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:40,620
into a much smaller task

157
00:07:40,620 --> 00:07:45,140
And these smaller tasks are a lot easier to train systems

158
00:07:45,140 --> 00:07:45,820
to do

159
00:07:45,820 --> 00:07:48,200
And if you can train systems to do the smaller tasks

160
00:07:48,200 --> 00:07:52,070
then you can try to compose them with a higher-level policy

161
00:07:52,070 --> 00:07:57,870
to be able to accomplish that more challenging task

162
00:07:57,870 --> 00:08:01,970
So what we can do is for the "bake a cheesecake" task

163
00:08:01,970 --> 00:08:05,190
we could pass this with a state or observation into a high-level

164
00:08:05,190 --> 00:08:09,190
policy, which is going to predict an intermediate step

165
00:08:09,190 --> 00:08:13,230
like for example, go to the store or go to the door

166
00:08:13,230 --> 00:08:15,590
And then that intermediate step, along with the state

167
00:08:15,590 --> 00:08:18,430
or observation, will be passed to the low-level policy

168
00:08:18,430 --> 00:08:22,150
to predict an action

169
00:08:22,150 --> 00:08:24,470
Now, a couple notes here

170
00:08:24,470 --> 00:08:30,670
So one note is that this low-level policy--

171
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:35,650
this needs to be acting at the original frequency

172
00:08:35,650 --> 00:08:39,590
So if you're controlling a robot

173
00:08:39,590 --> 00:08:41,330
maybe this is operating at 20 hertz

174
00:08:41,330 --> 00:08:42,950
If you're controlling a language model

175
00:08:42,950 --> 00:08:45,670
then this is going to be outputting word by word

176
00:08:45,670 --> 00:08:47,270
Whereas the high-level policy, it

177
00:08:47,270 --> 00:08:50,810
could actually predict goals for the low-level policy

178
00:08:50,810 --> 00:08:53,010
at a coarser timescale

179
00:08:53,010 --> 00:08:55,690
And so, for example, it could be outputting goals

180
00:08:55,690 --> 00:08:58,610
after you've completed a paragraph for a language model

181
00:08:58,610 --> 00:09:01,530
or after you've completed that--

182
00:09:01,530 --> 00:09:03,950
like going to the store, for example

183
00:09:03,950 --> 00:09:06,810
It could then give a new goal for the low-level policy

184
00:09:06,810 --> 00:09:08,380
to perform

185
00:09:08,380 --> 00:09:10,010
And so we have a high-level policy that

186
00:09:10,010 --> 00:09:11,350
predicts intermediate goals

187
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:12,810
And then the low level policy tries

188
00:09:12,810 --> 00:09:17,290
to accomplish those intermediate goals by predicting actions

189
00:09:17,290 --> 00:09:18,930
Typically, the low-level policy is

190
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:23,490
operating at a higher frequency than the high-level policy

191
00:09:23,490 --> 00:09:26,500
And I should note that--

192
00:09:26,500 --> 00:09:30,010
I'll refer to these as kind of goals or subtasks

193
00:09:30,010 --> 00:09:32,290
There's a number of other names for these

194
00:09:32,290 --> 00:09:34,690
as well, like skills or options

195
00:09:34,690 --> 00:09:37,270
You could also think about these as high-level actions

196
00:09:37,270 --> 00:09:39,850
So these are actions but for a different policy

197
00:09:39,850 --> 00:09:42,450
that's operating at a higher level of abstraction

198
00:09:42,450 --> 00:09:47,330
than the original policy

199
00:09:47,330 --> 00:09:49,460
And then the other thing here is that--

200
00:09:49,460 --> 00:09:51,820
this is like a two-level hierarchy

201
00:09:51,820 --> 00:09:55,260
but you could also imagine having more than two levels

202
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,800
of hierarchy as well

203
00:09:56,800 --> 00:09:59,500
And for example, the baking a cheesecake, you could have--

204
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:02,380
maybe one level of hierarchy could

205
00:10:02,380 --> 00:10:08,920
be things like go to the store, buy the things, bring them back

206
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:10,340
And a different level of hierarchy

207
00:10:10,340 --> 00:10:13,420
might actually be controlling, like the motors on a robot

208
00:10:13,420 --> 00:10:17,700
in order to accomplish those tasks

209
00:10:17,700 --> 00:10:20,520
And then once we have this hierarchical policy

210
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:23,800
how do we actually roll it out if we can do these things

211
00:10:23,800 --> 00:10:29,340
So specifically, if we're at, say, we'll start at t equals 1

212
00:10:29,340 --> 00:10:35,220
then the first step is to predict

213
00:10:35,220 --> 00:10:39,440
Or another way to think about predicting a goal is planning

214
00:10:39,440 --> 00:10:41,150
We're trying to think forward

215
00:10:41,150 --> 00:10:42,820
What is the next step that we should do

216
00:10:42,820 --> 00:10:45,140
So we could try to plan a goal

217
00:10:45,140 --> 00:10:50,280
And this will be by sampling from our high-level policy based

218
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:59,000
off of the current state and optionally some high-level goal

219
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,160
And then we'll be repeatedly sampling

220
00:11:02,160 --> 00:11:04,480
from our low-level policy

221
00:11:04,480 --> 00:11:08,960
And so we could repeat

222
00:11:08,960 --> 00:11:16,240
First sample, an action from the low-level policy using this goal

223
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:17,360
as input

224
00:11:17,360 --> 00:11:27,400
So sample a from high low level, with the state and this goal--

225
00:11:27,400 --> 00:11:29,640
the goal from the high level as input

226
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:33,280
And then we'll actually execute that action

227
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:39,920
and get the next state

228
00:11:39,920 --> 00:11:42,770
So basically at that point, we'll then--

229
00:11:42,770 --> 00:11:46,210
t will now become t plus 1

230
00:11:46,210 --> 00:11:51,250
And we'll observe St. Then again with the same goal as before

231
00:11:51,250 --> 00:11:54,250
But the new state will then be sampling a new action

232
00:11:54,250 --> 00:11:56,650
repeating this process

233
00:11:56,650 --> 00:11:59,250
And then at some point after we have--

234
00:11:59,250 --> 00:12:01,010
and we'll talk a little bit about how we

235
00:12:01,010 --> 00:12:02,420
decide when to get a new goal

236
00:12:02,420 --> 00:12:04,170
But when we have decided that we're either

237
00:12:04,170 --> 00:12:06,530
done with the goal or enough time has passed

238
00:12:06,530 --> 00:12:11,610
then we'll move back to step one to re-plan a new goal based off

239
00:12:11,610 --> 00:12:14,010
of the latest state

240
00:12:14,010 --> 00:12:16,310
Yeah

241
00:12:16,310 --> 00:12:18,890
So let's walk through this, and then I'll get to your question

242
00:12:18,890 --> 00:12:19,470
in a minute

243
00:12:19,470 --> 00:12:23,410
So is there some initial state or observation, plan a new goal

244
00:12:23,410 --> 00:12:24,890
Until a new goal is selected, we're

245
00:12:24,890 --> 00:12:27,410
going to execute a predicted action according

246
00:12:27,410 --> 00:12:30,650
to our low-level policy based off of that predicted goal

247
00:12:30,650 --> 00:12:34,050
And then we'll observe a new observation

248
00:12:34,050 --> 00:12:36,630
Now, why might hierarchy help

249
00:12:36,630 --> 00:12:39,050
Which I think is getting at your question

250
00:12:39,050 --> 00:12:40,990
There's a couple reasons for this

251
00:12:40,990 --> 00:12:45,390
So the first is that typically we're

252
00:12:45,390 --> 00:12:47,790
going to be training this low-level policy to reach

253
00:12:47,790 --> 00:12:51,430
goals and this high-level policy to maximize rewards or reach

254
00:12:51,430 --> 00:12:52,950
some high-level goals

255
00:12:52,950 --> 00:12:57,110
And this means that the objective for this policy

256
00:12:57,110 --> 00:13:00,470
is different than the overall objective of completing

257
00:13:00,470 --> 00:13:02,030
the long-horizon task

258
00:13:02,030 --> 00:13:04,290
And so this is actually getting different supervision

259
00:13:04,290 --> 00:13:05,960
And this intermediate supervision signal

260
00:13:05,960 --> 00:13:08,790
for how to complete intermediate goals or tasks

261
00:13:08,790 --> 00:13:11,910
can be useful for guiding the policy towards completing

262
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:13,350
the long-horizon task

263
00:13:13,350 --> 00:13:14,950
So you can think about it potentially

264
00:13:14,950 --> 00:13:17,070
It depends on the setup, but you can think about it

265
00:13:17,070 --> 00:13:20,110
as a way to leverage intermediate supervision

266
00:13:20,110 --> 00:13:22,190
Now you could always have intermediate supervision

267
00:13:22,190 --> 00:13:24,110
when training a big, flat policy as well, where

268
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:25,900
you give it shaped rewards, and you give it

269
00:13:25,900 --> 00:13:28,880
rewards for completing individual subtasks

270
00:13:28,880 --> 00:13:30,630
But compared to that, the other difference

271
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:34,350
here is that this low-level multi-task policy

272
00:13:34,350 --> 00:13:38,990
is going to be able to more explicitly share knowledge

273
00:13:38,990 --> 00:13:41,350
across the different subtasks

274
00:13:41,350 --> 00:13:47,440
So because, for example, it has this goal as input

275
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:51,800
it's targeting a particular subtask

276
00:13:51,800 --> 00:13:55,880
And if two subtasks are quite similar to one another

277
00:13:55,880 --> 00:14:01,840
then this policy will be able to share data across those tasks

278
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:03,420
in order to more efficiently learn

279
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:04,880
compared to if you were just to try

280
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:08,640
to train a single policy across the long-horizon task

281
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,680
In reinforcement learning, specifically, you

282
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:14,680
can also think about benefits in terms of exploration

283
00:14:14,680 --> 00:14:18,920
And in particular, when this high-level policy

284
00:14:18,920 --> 00:14:21,320
is proposing goals to reach, that's

285
00:14:21,320 --> 00:14:23,000
structuring the exploration process

286
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:26,040
to encourage it to explore not just in the original action

287
00:14:26,040 --> 00:14:30,920
space but also in this higher-level goal space

288
00:14:30,920 --> 00:14:35,960
And lastly, there can also be sometimes practical benefits

289
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:36,460
as well

290
00:14:36,460 --> 00:14:37,920
So, for example, if you're thinking

291
00:14:37,920 --> 00:14:40,650
about this task of making a cheesecake, for example

292
00:14:40,650 --> 00:14:42,980
you might need to be reasoning through the steps for how

293
00:14:42,980 --> 00:14:43,960
to do that

294
00:14:43,960 --> 00:14:46,580
And if you have a single policy, then you

295
00:14:46,580 --> 00:14:48,300
need to reason through those steps

296
00:14:48,300 --> 00:14:52,580
every single timestep with that flat policy

297
00:14:52,580 --> 00:14:54,600
So if your robot is operating at 20 hertz

298
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:57,100
for example, you're going to be reasoning through like, what

299
00:14:57,100 --> 00:14:58,560
is the next thing I should be doing

300
00:14:58,560 --> 00:15:02,140
and the way to get there with my action at every single timestep

301
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:05,260
Where here, we can run this reasoning computation

302
00:15:05,260 --> 00:15:09,340
at a lower frequency than the low-level policy

303
00:15:09,340 --> 00:15:11,340
So that can be kind of a practical benefit

304
00:15:11,340 --> 00:15:13,640
in terms of compute costs

305
00:15:13,640 --> 00:15:14,140
Yeah

306
00:15:14,140 --> 00:15:17,380
So the question is, can we elaborate on the first point

307
00:15:17,380 --> 00:15:19,580
and how the supervision for the low-level policy

308
00:15:19,580 --> 00:15:21,420
is different for the high-level policy

309
00:15:21,420 --> 00:15:23,940
So the high level policy--

310
00:15:23,940 --> 00:15:26,860
it's goal is to complete the long-horizon task

311
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:29,940
So it's goal is to bake the cheesecake

312
00:15:29,940 --> 00:15:32,380
and it gets rewards when the cheesecake is baked

313
00:15:32,380 --> 00:15:35,980
The low-level policy-- it gets rewards

314
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:38,460
for completing intermediate subtasks

315
00:15:38,460 --> 00:15:42,850
And so when it, for example, finds the ingredients, preheat

316
00:15:42,850 --> 00:15:44,270
the oven, that sort of thing, it's

317
00:15:44,270 --> 00:15:46,990
getting rewards for completing those tasks

318
00:15:46,990 --> 00:15:48,990
And that intermediate supervision

319
00:15:48,990 --> 00:15:51,970
guides it towards completing the long-horizon task

320
00:15:51,970 --> 00:15:52,470
Yeah

321
00:15:52,470 --> 00:15:54,220
So the question is, what if you need to be

322
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:56,310
doing multiple tasks at once

323
00:15:56,310 --> 00:16:02,620
So in that case, unless your low-level agent can has--

324
00:16:02,620 --> 00:16:04,950
I don't know-- 10 arms or something or can output things

325
00:16:04,950 --> 00:16:08,830
in parallel, you do need to pick some order for that

326
00:16:08,830 --> 00:16:11,870
Now, the goal of the high-level policy

327
00:16:11,870 --> 00:16:14,450
is to figure out whether that order matters or not

328
00:16:14,450 --> 00:16:18,170
And so if it figures out that the order is really important

329
00:16:18,170 --> 00:16:20,350
then it will get rewards

330
00:16:20,350 --> 00:16:22,470
It will be better able to predict those goals if it

331
00:16:22,470 --> 00:16:23,990
predicts the correct order

332
00:16:23,990 --> 00:16:26,330
In other circumstances, if the order doesn't matter

333
00:16:26,330 --> 00:16:31,430
then it should be able to learn that and figure out

334
00:16:31,430 --> 00:16:33,750
that it doesn't really matter, and it'll just

335
00:16:33,750 --> 00:16:36,910
pick one arbitrarily

336
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:40,960
Now, there's also a question of whether or not

337
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:43,040
we actually need multiple policies in order

338
00:16:43,040 --> 00:16:45,120
to get some of these benefits

339
00:16:45,120 --> 00:16:48,840
And on the last slide, we talked about the benefits

340
00:16:48,840 --> 00:16:51,320
of what you might get with a hierarchical approach

341
00:16:51,320 --> 00:16:54,760
versus just what's called a flat policy with no hierarchy, which

342
00:16:54,760 --> 00:16:59,440
is the typical path in the long-horizon task and the state

343
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:01,680
and predict the action

344
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:03,680
There's actually also an intermediate approach

345
00:17:03,680 --> 00:17:06,440
that you can take as well, that actually

346
00:17:06,440 --> 00:17:08,680
retains a lot of the benefits of hierarchy

347
00:17:08,680 --> 00:17:11,079
and actually, in some ways, has a lot of the same design

348
00:17:11,079 --> 00:17:14,520
choices, which is something that's a little bit like chain

349
00:17:14,520 --> 00:17:18,280
of thought in language models, if you're familiar with that--

350
00:17:18,280 --> 00:17:21,520
which is that if you have some long-horizon task

351
00:17:21,520 --> 00:17:26,319
you could have the policy first reason through what goal

352
00:17:26,319 --> 00:17:29,800
or what subtask it should be completing and then predict

353
00:17:29,800 --> 00:17:31,400
the low-level action

354
00:17:31,400 --> 00:17:36,960
So this is kind of combining these two policies into one

355
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:39,080
But now you're just predicting them in sequence

356
00:17:39,080 --> 00:17:42,140
You're first predicting the goal and then the action

357
00:17:42,140 --> 00:17:43,580
This sort of approach can actually

358
00:17:43,580 --> 00:17:45,520
retain a fair number of the benefits

359
00:17:45,520 --> 00:17:47,020
so it can benefit from the same sort

360
00:17:47,020 --> 00:17:49,460
of intermediate supervision

361
00:17:49,460 --> 00:17:51,560
It also might have some practical downsides

362
00:17:51,560 --> 00:17:53,740
So this doesn't have the benefits

363
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:56,580
of the kind of practical computational benefits

364
00:17:56,580 --> 00:17:58,660
of separating them into two because then you still

365
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:02,020
have to run this at the full frequency

366
00:18:02,020 --> 00:18:04,540
There isn't entirely a conclusion

367
00:18:04,540 --> 00:18:07,940
yet empirically on whether this is just

368
00:18:07,940 --> 00:18:09,560
as good as a hierarchical approach

369
00:18:09,560 --> 00:18:11,140
And you could imagine that there might

370
00:18:11,140 --> 00:18:13,660
be benefits in terms of sharing some knowledge

371
00:18:13,660 --> 00:18:16,580
with low-level policies versus with a combined approach

372
00:18:16,580 --> 00:18:18,300
So this is an open area of research

373
00:18:18,300 --> 00:18:20,700
on whether or not you could just do this

374
00:18:20,700 --> 00:18:22,740
And the only really conclusive downside

375
00:18:22,740 --> 00:18:28,540
would be the computational upside of having reasoning

376
00:18:28,540 --> 00:18:32,600
at a lower frequency than taking actions for the first one

377
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:35,020
So the question is, why is having intermediate supervision

378
00:18:35,020 --> 00:18:36,950
helpful

379
00:18:36,950 --> 00:18:38,530
There's a couple reasons

380
00:18:38,530 --> 00:18:40,590
So if you're in a reinforcement learning setting

381
00:18:40,590 --> 00:18:42,470
and your goal is to make a cheesecake

382
00:18:42,470 --> 00:18:46,070
and you only get rewards when the cheesecake is baked versus

383
00:18:46,070 --> 00:18:49,190
not, then those rewards are going to be really sparse

384
00:18:49,190 --> 00:18:52,290
and not actually allow you to progress

385
00:18:52,290 --> 00:18:54,790
The second is that even if you-- say, you're in an imitation

386
00:18:54,790 --> 00:19:02,110
learning setting, like you're just predicting actions

387
00:19:02,110 --> 00:19:04,310
imitating actions for making a cheesecake

388
00:19:04,310 --> 00:19:08,740
then sometimes if the policy isn't explicitly

389
00:19:08,740 --> 00:19:11,070
thinking about what part of that task it might be doing

390
00:19:11,070 --> 00:19:14,370
it might oscillate between different subtasks

391
00:19:14,370 --> 00:19:18,950
especially if it doesn't have a history of past observations

392
00:19:18,950 --> 00:19:23,070
So there's also even benefits in imitation learning settings

393
00:19:23,070 --> 00:19:24,750
as well

394
00:19:24,750 --> 00:19:27,430
So now let's dive into some of the design choices

395
00:19:27,430 --> 00:19:30,190
This will also start to answer some of the questions that

396
00:19:30,190 --> 00:19:31,870
have come up as well

397
00:19:31,870 --> 00:19:35,470
So the first is that we've abstractly

398
00:19:35,470 --> 00:19:38,450
been talking about these different goals

399
00:19:38,450 --> 00:19:42,050
and we need to think about how to actually represent

400
00:19:42,050 --> 00:19:44,450
those different goals

401
00:19:44,450 --> 00:19:49,490
And I'm actually want to start with this as an exercise for you

402
00:19:49,490 --> 00:19:53,770
all to think about what different representations might

403
00:19:53,770 --> 00:19:55,740
make sense for different problems

404
00:19:55,740 --> 00:19:57,990
And so I'll give you a few different example problems

405
00:19:57,990 --> 00:20:00,290
And then the goal for you is to think through

406
00:20:00,290 --> 00:20:02,090
for that particular problem

407
00:20:02,090 --> 00:20:05,130
What would be kind of sensible intermediate steps

408
00:20:05,130 --> 00:20:06,790
for the agent

409
00:20:06,790 --> 00:20:08,290
And how would you actually represent

410
00:20:08,290 --> 00:20:10,290
those different intermediate steps

411
00:20:10,290 --> 00:20:13,050
So the first task is a robot that

412
00:20:13,050 --> 00:20:17,170
wants to learn how to cook Italian dishes like focaccia

413
00:20:17,170 --> 00:20:20,450
What would the intermediate steps for those cooking tasks

414
00:20:20,450 --> 00:20:23,410
look like if it needs to control its motors to complete

415
00:20:23,410 --> 00:20:26,450
that task and the representation for that

416
00:20:26,450 --> 00:20:30,490
The second one is maybe you're developing an autonomous driving

417
00:20:30,490 --> 00:20:35,740
bike, and you want it to break down navigation and biking

418
00:20:35,740 --> 00:20:38,460
to various locations on campus

419
00:20:38,460 --> 00:20:40,540
The third is a language model that

420
00:20:40,540 --> 00:20:44,300
is drafting legal briefs, which is also a rather

421
00:20:44,300 --> 00:20:46,160
complex and involved process

422
00:20:46,160 --> 00:20:48,340
And then the last is maybe a web agent

423
00:20:48,340 --> 00:20:54,140
that needs to plan and book one week vacations for users

424
00:20:54,140 --> 00:20:57,080
So I'll give you a couple minutes to pick one of these

425
00:20:57,080 --> 00:20:59,660
Discuss with your neighbor what intermediate goals would look

426
00:20:59,660 --> 00:21:00,340
like

427
00:21:00,340 --> 00:21:05,500
And then we'll come back and discuss

428
00:21:05,500 --> 00:21:08,120
Does anyone want to share what they discussed

429
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:08,620
Cool

430
00:21:08,620 --> 00:21:09,120
Yeah

431
00:21:09,120 --> 00:21:10,900
So for number two, one way that you

432
00:21:10,900 --> 00:21:13,070
could address it is the different intermediate goals

433
00:21:13,070 --> 00:21:18,100
could be like waypoints towards that particular goal location

434
00:21:18,100 --> 00:21:19,160
that you want to reach

435
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:21,760
And then the representation for that could be GPS coordinates

436
00:21:21,760 --> 00:21:23,380
And the reward for the low-level policy

437
00:21:23,380 --> 00:21:26,700
would be like, how close are you to those GPS coordinates

438
00:21:26,700 --> 00:21:28,980
So yeah, something like that makes sense

439
00:21:28,980 --> 00:21:32,980
Anyone else want to share either for that problem

440
00:21:32,980 --> 00:21:34,880
or a different problem

441
00:21:34,880 --> 00:21:35,580
Yeah, cool

442
00:21:35,580 --> 00:21:37,680
So for the fourth one, you could-- there's

443
00:21:37,680 --> 00:21:39,020
a few different ways you could think about it

444
00:21:39,020 --> 00:21:41,390
You could think about it as intermediate goals or booking

445
00:21:41,390 --> 00:21:43,440
day one, booking day two, booking day three

446
00:21:43,440 --> 00:21:46,020
Or you could have a more coarse, defined approach where you're

447
00:21:46,020 --> 00:21:49,400
booking travel, booking--

448
00:21:49,400 --> 00:21:51,440
I don't know-- hotels or whatever, and then

449
00:21:51,440 --> 00:21:52,920
specific activities

450
00:21:52,920 --> 00:21:54,880
One other part of this that I would imagine you

451
00:21:54,880 --> 00:21:57,040
would need to do is just generally figuring out

452
00:21:57,040 --> 00:21:59,140
what does the user want from their vacation

453
00:21:59,140 --> 00:22:02,200
And so doing high-level research first

454
00:22:02,200 --> 00:22:05,200
And that could be also an intermediate goal

455
00:22:05,200 --> 00:22:08,080
as part of completing the task

456
00:22:08,080 --> 00:22:10,600
Did anyone pick one or three

457
00:22:10,600 --> 00:22:13,180
So the intermediate goals are things like opening the cabinet

458
00:22:13,180 --> 00:22:21,790
beating the eggs, and then the representation for those goals

459
00:22:21,790 --> 00:22:22,540
All right

460
00:22:22,540 --> 00:22:24,070
Didn't quite get to that

461
00:22:24,070 --> 00:22:25,440
So a few things that you could do

462
00:22:25,440 --> 00:22:26,920
for the representation for that is it

463
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:28,300
could just be a natural language

464
00:22:28,300 --> 00:22:31,040
So it could be literally the string beat the eggs

465
00:22:31,040 --> 00:22:32,970
or open the cabinet

466
00:22:32,970 --> 00:22:35,040
If, for example, if you're cooking something

467
00:22:35,040 --> 00:22:36,830
where you want to look in a particular way

468
00:22:36,830 --> 00:22:40,210
you can even have an image as an intermediate goal representation

469
00:22:40,210 --> 00:22:43,730
as well, saying like, I want the bread to look like this

470
00:22:43,730 --> 00:22:45,470
or yeah, that sort of thing

471
00:22:45,470 --> 00:22:50,930
So can imagine multimodal goal representations as well

472
00:22:50,930 --> 00:22:53,930
So one of the things that I wanted

473
00:22:53,930 --> 00:22:57,410
to convey with this exercise is that oftentimes the goal

474
00:22:57,410 --> 00:23:01,490
representation that you might pick likely

475
00:23:01,490 --> 00:23:04,460
depends on the particular domain or the particular problem

476
00:23:04,460 --> 00:23:06,170
For example, like for biking, it might be

477
00:23:06,170 --> 00:23:07,850
something like GPS coordinates

478
00:23:07,850 --> 00:23:10,210
Whereas for cooking, it might be language

479
00:23:10,210 --> 00:23:13,690
For booking a vacation, it might also be in language

480
00:23:13,690 --> 00:23:16,890
There might also be images involved and so forth

481
00:23:16,890 --> 00:23:20,890
And in terms of what these representations might look like

482
00:23:20,890 --> 00:23:24,250
oftentimes you'll want it to be--

483
00:23:24,250 --> 00:23:27,290
if you're developing something for many different subtasks

484
00:23:27,290 --> 00:23:28,790
you want it to be fairly expressive

485
00:23:28,790 --> 00:23:32,200
If you're trying to cook lots of different meals--

486
00:23:32,200 --> 00:23:34,620
natural language is something that's like quite expressive

487
00:23:34,620 --> 00:23:39,420
and can describe a number of different low-level behaviors

488
00:23:39,420 --> 00:23:42,100
Ideally, it also has some structure

489
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:44,820
And so for example, if your different goals are just

490
00:23:44,820 --> 00:23:47,540
do task subtask one, do subtask two

491
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:49,580
it's going to be hard for your low-level policy

492
00:23:49,580 --> 00:23:52,020
to figure out how those relate to one another

493
00:23:52,020 --> 00:23:54,380
Whereas if you have something like GPS coordinates

494
00:23:54,380 --> 00:23:59,020
then two GPS coordinates that are very close to each other

495
00:23:59,020 --> 00:24:00,520
are going to have similar actions

496
00:24:00,520 --> 00:24:02,820
And that sort of structure will make

497
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:08,100
the learning problem a lot easier for the low-level policy

498
00:24:08,100 --> 00:24:10,820
And then the third is also picking an appropriate level

499
00:24:10,820 --> 00:24:12,020
of abstraction

500
00:24:12,020 --> 00:24:17,300
So for the biking task, for example

501
00:24:17,300 --> 00:24:19,380
you can have a level of abstraction that's

502
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:22,160
like potentially two low level

503
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:26,860
So you could have something that's kind of pedal--

504
00:24:26,860 --> 00:24:30,200
I don't know-- current, like exact velocity

505
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:31,400
for the next second

506
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:33,280
You could also have something too high level

507
00:24:33,280 --> 00:24:37,120
which is like go to the quad or something, which might not

508
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:39,720
be particularly helpful in terms of breaking the problem down

509
00:24:39,720 --> 00:24:41,280
into smaller steps

510
00:24:41,280 --> 00:24:43,960
And choosing the right level of abstraction

511
00:24:43,960 --> 00:24:50,220
is often somewhat tricky and can also be domain dependent

512
00:24:50,220 --> 00:24:53,120
depending on the particular kind of details

513
00:24:53,120 --> 00:24:57,130
of the problem and the application area

514
00:24:57,130 --> 00:24:58,920
So the question is, should we also abstract

515
00:24:58,920 --> 00:25:02,440
the state space for each layer

516
00:25:02,440 --> 00:25:07,480
This depends on whether or not you have access to that--

517
00:25:07,480 --> 00:25:14,160
that sort of abstraction layer

518
00:25:14,160 --> 00:25:14,860
Let's see

519
00:25:14,860 --> 00:25:17,000
Oftentimes, even for the high-level policy

520
00:25:17,000 --> 00:25:19,080
it's helpful for it to have all of the information

521
00:25:19,080 --> 00:25:23,920
about the current state in order to make even high-level goal

522
00:25:23,920 --> 00:25:26,000
predictions

523
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,830
But yeah, there are some things that you can do

524
00:25:30,830 --> 00:25:32,850
You could, for example, ask a language model

525
00:25:32,850 --> 00:25:35,850
to summarize the high-level information about a state

526
00:25:35,850 --> 00:25:39,490
and have a high-level policy use that information

527
00:25:39,490 --> 00:25:43,610
But generally, in practice, you'll

528
00:25:43,610 --> 00:25:47,510
often use the same low level, like the full-state information

529
00:25:47,510 --> 00:25:50,490
You don't lose too much from doing that as well

530
00:25:50,490 --> 00:25:52,530
Now let's talk a little bit about the supervision

531
00:25:52,530 --> 00:25:53,430
for each level

532
00:25:53,430 --> 00:25:59,410
And for this, I think it's helpful to consider an example

533
00:25:59,410 --> 00:26:08,050
So let's consider an example where you're making pasta

534
00:26:08,050 --> 00:26:12,440
And I chose this to be slightly simpler long-horizon task

535
00:26:12,440 --> 00:26:14,690
And you could imagine there being a few steps involved

536
00:26:14,690 --> 00:26:16,090
for doing this

537
00:26:16,090 --> 00:26:19,090
And we can abstractly think about the state

538
00:26:19,090 --> 00:26:22,220
space for this task and reaching different intermediate steps

539
00:26:22,220 --> 00:26:26,870
for this

540
00:26:26,870 --> 00:26:27,370
Let's see

541
00:26:27,370 --> 00:26:32,430
Let's say that this is your initial state distribution

542
00:26:32,430 --> 00:26:35,030
And the steps for making pasta is

543
00:26:35,030 --> 00:26:39,750
that you'll need to get out a pot and pasta, so

544
00:26:39,750 --> 00:26:40,710
some ingredients

545
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:42,570
You'll need to boil the water

546
00:26:42,570 --> 00:26:49,830
And you also need to pour the pasta into the boiling water

547
00:26:49,830 --> 00:26:51,330
Maybe there's also a straining step

548
00:26:51,330 --> 00:26:53,470
but maybe we can skip that for now

549
00:26:53,470 --> 00:26:59,390
And we can think about the--

550
00:26:59,390 --> 00:27:02,690
imagine the set of states where you have--

551
00:27:02,690 --> 00:27:06,930
for example, you have taken out a pot

552
00:27:06,930 --> 00:27:13,890
So find a pot before actually like putting any water into it

553
00:27:13,890 --> 00:27:16,310
You could have some states, where

554
00:27:16,310 --> 00:27:23,070
you've now have boiled the water, another set of states

555
00:27:23,070 --> 00:27:32,400
where you have found the pasta, and then some other states where

556
00:27:32,400 --> 00:27:42,040
the pasta is cooked

557
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:43,920
If we have our low-level policy, it's

558
00:27:43,920 --> 00:27:46,320
going to be trying to figure out like

559
00:27:46,320 --> 00:27:49,700
how do I take actions to reach these kinds of states

560
00:27:49,700 --> 00:27:51,080
And then the high-level policy is

561
00:27:51,080 --> 00:27:54,560
trying to predict, if I'm in a given state

562
00:27:54,560 --> 00:27:59,040
what command should I use to tell the low-level policy what

563
00:27:59,040 --> 00:28:00,400
to do

564
00:28:00,400 --> 00:28:02,520
And generally, as we've talked about a bit

565
00:28:02,520 --> 00:28:06,080
before, for the low-level policy

566
00:28:06,080 --> 00:28:08,320
it's going to be getting rewards that are based off

567
00:28:08,320 --> 00:28:11,560
of the low-level tasks

568
00:28:11,560 --> 00:28:14,440
And the goal of something like this

569
00:28:14,440 --> 00:28:18,220
would be something where you're moving towards finding a pot

570
00:28:18,220 --> 00:28:22,780
Maybe then you try to boil the water

571
00:28:22,780 --> 00:28:24,380
Then you try to find the pasta

572
00:28:24,380 --> 00:28:27,700
And then you try to cook the pasta

573
00:28:27,700 --> 00:28:30,720
Now, one thing that we talked about a little bit before is--

574
00:28:30,720 --> 00:28:33,300
so this is actually deviating from this example a little bit--

575
00:28:33,300 --> 00:28:34,900
we'll come back to it--

576
00:28:34,900 --> 00:28:38,100
is we could also just train the high-level policy

577
00:28:38,100 --> 00:28:40,420
and the low-level policy end to end

578
00:28:40,420 --> 00:28:43,800
without any intermediate goal representation

579
00:28:43,800 --> 00:28:45,340
Just for example, we'd have some sort

580
00:28:45,340 --> 00:28:46,920
of latent goal representation

581
00:28:46,920 --> 00:28:49,340
That's just the activations of the neural network

582
00:28:49,340 --> 00:28:52,300
And we could train all of these just

583
00:28:52,300 --> 00:28:57,260
for the high-level task and the rewards of completing the task

584
00:28:57,260 --> 00:28:59,580
Now, one important thing that is good to know about

585
00:28:59,580 --> 00:29:03,140
of the supervision is that if you do that, that's basically

586
00:29:03,140 --> 00:29:07,820
just a flat policy with a particular architecture that

587
00:29:07,820 --> 00:29:12,020
has some sort of activations when you feed in the observation

588
00:29:12,020 --> 00:29:12,520
twice

589
00:29:12,520 --> 00:29:14,380
You just think about that as basically

590
00:29:14,380 --> 00:29:16,900
a single neural network architecture

591
00:29:16,900 --> 00:29:21,500
That's basically the same as a flat policy

592
00:29:21,500 --> 00:29:24,390
And the reason why it's important to recognize this is

593
00:29:24,390 --> 00:29:27,250
is that when designing these kinds of systems

594
00:29:27,250 --> 00:29:29,710
it's important to think about where the benefits are coming

595
00:29:29,710 --> 00:29:32,010
from, whether it's coming from additional supervision

596
00:29:32,010 --> 00:29:34,910
whether it's coming from structured exploration

597
00:29:34,910 --> 00:29:37,010
And if you don't think carefully about this

598
00:29:37,010 --> 00:29:39,040
you could end up in a situation where

599
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:41,540
intuitively if you haven't thought about it, it's like, oh

600
00:29:41,540 --> 00:29:43,330
Ideally, my high-level policy will do this

601
00:29:43,330 --> 00:29:46,390
My low-level policy will do this without actually seeing benefits

602
00:29:46,390 --> 00:29:48,710
compared to just naively training

603
00:29:48,710 --> 00:29:53,260
things with a single policy

604
00:29:53,260 --> 00:29:54,970
For those of you that are doing research

605
00:29:54,970 --> 00:29:57,470
it's also just generally something that's good to do

606
00:29:57,470 --> 00:30:00,100
in any kind of research because you never really know

607
00:30:00,100 --> 00:30:02,470
if the systems that you're developing or the methods that

608
00:30:02,470 --> 00:30:05,670
you're developing are going to improve over some existing

609
00:30:05,670 --> 00:30:06,920
state-of-the-art method

610
00:30:06,920 --> 00:30:08,550
And if you're very honest with yourself

611
00:30:08,550 --> 00:30:13,270
about what might go wrong and why might not actually

612
00:30:13,270 --> 00:30:15,990
see benefits from your new idea, then you

613
00:30:15,990 --> 00:30:18,310
can think more critically about what sorts of things

614
00:30:18,310 --> 00:30:22,410
will actually improve the final results

615
00:30:22,410 --> 00:30:24,410
Cool

616
00:30:24,410 --> 00:30:25,750
So supervising each level

617
00:30:25,750 --> 00:30:28,010
So we have the low-level policy

618
00:30:28,010 --> 00:30:30,210
And the low-level policy will be trained

619
00:30:30,210 --> 00:30:34,610
to accomplish that goal, not the original task

620
00:30:34,610 --> 00:30:38,710
And so when you're passing in the string of "find pot,"

621
00:30:38,710 --> 00:30:42,090
it's going to be getting a reward that is trying

622
00:30:42,090 --> 00:30:46,370
to maximize reward for getting to the states where you found

623
00:30:46,370 --> 00:30:48,250
the pot

624
00:30:48,250 --> 00:30:51,050
And the high-level policy--

625
00:30:51,050 --> 00:30:54,090
its goal is to predict the subtasks

626
00:30:54,090 --> 00:31:01,490
so that you end up in the final state of the pasta being cooked

627
00:31:01,490 --> 00:31:05,810
So this is kind of trying to accomplish the original goal

628
00:31:05,810 --> 00:31:08,290
Now there's a couple of things that can be

629
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:10,390
a little bit tricky about this

630
00:31:10,390 --> 00:31:13,690
So say that you're training these separately

631
00:31:13,690 --> 00:31:17,890
and say that you've trained--

632
00:31:17,890 --> 00:31:20,290
you could, for example, think about training a policy

633
00:31:20,290 --> 00:31:22,860
to find the pot, and maybe you've

634
00:31:22,860 --> 00:31:26,370
gotten to a good policy that's really good at finding the pot

635
00:31:26,370 --> 00:31:33,820
and getting to the state really reliably

636
00:31:33,820 --> 00:31:37,580
And then maybe for example, when you train the policy

637
00:31:37,580 --> 00:31:43,150
for finding the pasta

638
00:31:43,150 --> 00:31:45,360
If you weren't exactly careful with how you did this

639
00:31:45,360 --> 00:31:47,860
maybe you would think about, OK, maybe I could actually just

640
00:31:47,860 --> 00:31:49,420
start from this state and also try

641
00:31:49,420 --> 00:31:53,450
to find the pasta from this state over here

642
00:31:53,450 --> 00:31:55,660
And that's not taking into account that actually when

643
00:31:55,660 --> 00:31:57,260
it's finding the pasta, according

644
00:31:57,260 --> 00:31:59,900
to the high-level policy, if the high-level policy actually

645
00:31:59,900 --> 00:32:03,860
wanted it to find the pasta after boiling the water when

646
00:32:03,860 --> 00:32:05,540
you're at the stove

647
00:32:05,540 --> 00:32:07,620
Then when you actually try to string together

648
00:32:07,620 --> 00:32:12,300
this low-level policy with the low-level policy for these two

649
00:32:12,300 --> 00:32:14,100
It'll actually then struggle to transition

650
00:32:14,100 --> 00:32:19,100
from going to the stove over to finding where the pasta is

651
00:32:19,100 --> 00:32:21,950
And this is basically like a mismatch when you're

652
00:32:21,950 --> 00:32:24,810
training these modular systems

653
00:32:24,810 --> 00:32:26,890
And you're training them completely independently

654
00:32:26,890 --> 00:32:30,270
You might not actually get a system that works effectively

655
00:32:30,270 --> 00:32:33,390
if they're not actually at some point connected together

656
00:32:33,390 --> 00:32:38,320
and trained such that this skill is starting from states

657
00:32:38,320 --> 00:32:40,030
where the high-level policy will actually

658
00:32:40,030 --> 00:32:42,950
start commanding that task

659
00:32:42,950 --> 00:32:47,990
And so essentially when you're training the low-level policy--

660
00:32:47,990 --> 00:32:52,350
so you have your low-level policy actions

661
00:32:52,350 --> 00:32:54,270
given states and goals

662
00:32:54,270 --> 00:32:58,230
You want this policy to work for the set of states

663
00:32:58,230 --> 00:33:00,990
where the high-level policy is going to command

664
00:33:00,990 --> 00:33:03,630
this particular goal

665
00:33:03,630 --> 00:33:07,260
Another way to think about this is that--

666
00:33:07,260 --> 00:33:08,940
and I guess the other thing here is also

667
00:33:08,940 --> 00:33:11,190
you want it to be able to pick the goals that actually

668
00:33:11,190 --> 00:33:13,410
the high-level policy would predict

669
00:33:13,410 --> 00:33:17,350
So for different distributions of goals

670
00:33:17,350 --> 00:33:20,370
and different distributions of states, ideally

671
00:33:20,370 --> 00:33:22,610
that low level policy would work for whatever

672
00:33:22,610 --> 00:33:28,170
states and goals the high-level policy will output

673
00:33:28,170 --> 00:33:30,670
Now, if we haven't actually learned a high-level policy yet

674
00:33:30,670 --> 00:33:35,090
this puts things in a bit of a tricky situation

675
00:33:35,090 --> 00:33:37,930
And likewise for the high-level policy

676
00:33:37,930 --> 00:33:42,450
if we assume that our low-level policy is really great

677
00:33:42,450 --> 00:33:47,210
then we might not realize that it actually

678
00:33:47,210 --> 00:33:49,710
has some deficiencies and that we need to, for example

679
00:33:49,710 --> 00:33:52,650
continue to command something in certain states

680
00:33:52,650 --> 00:33:55,530
until you've actually reached a state that a different skill can

681
00:33:55,530 --> 00:33:57,130
operate in

682
00:33:57,130 --> 00:33:58,090
Yeah

683
00:33:58,090 --> 00:34:00,750
So if the low-level policy works from particular sequences

684
00:34:00,750 --> 00:34:02,290
of states, then the high-level policy

685
00:34:02,290 --> 00:34:05,490
can learn that it should walk the low-level policy

686
00:34:05,490 --> 00:34:06,510
through those states

687
00:34:06,510 --> 00:34:08,670
So you don't necessarily need your low-level policy

688
00:34:08,670 --> 00:34:09,710
to work from every state

689
00:34:09,710 --> 00:34:12,130
It just needs to work for the state-goal combinations

690
00:34:12,130 --> 00:34:14,449
that the high-level policy is going to command

691
00:34:14,449 --> 00:34:17,580
Now, you might have a situation where

692
00:34:17,580 --> 00:34:21,860
say, that your low-level policy knows

693
00:34:21,860 --> 00:34:23,920
how to do something like this

694
00:34:23,920 --> 00:34:26,040
So it knows how to go from here to here

695
00:34:26,040 --> 00:34:27,540
It knows how to go from here to here

696
00:34:27,540 --> 00:34:31,739
and maybe knows how to go from here to here

697
00:34:31,739 --> 00:34:33,340
like with some boiled water

698
00:34:33,340 --> 00:34:34,940
Then you might have some sort of gap

699
00:34:34,940 --> 00:34:36,920
where to actually find the pasta

700
00:34:36,920 --> 00:34:39,100
you need to go back to these states

701
00:34:39,100 --> 00:34:42,699
And if you don't have a command or some way

702
00:34:42,699 --> 00:34:45,100
to get from here to here, then you're

703
00:34:45,100 --> 00:34:47,280
going to basically have a gap in the states

704
00:34:47,280 --> 00:34:48,239
that you're visited in

705
00:34:48,239 --> 00:34:50,420
The high-level policy will struggle to actually

706
00:34:50,420 --> 00:34:52,300
get to this state here

707
00:34:52,300 --> 00:34:53,820
And so basically, one way to think

708
00:34:53,820 --> 00:34:56,580
about it is you need to make sure that there's at least some

709
00:34:56,580 --> 00:35:00,500
overlap in the states from one skill to the next

710
00:35:00,500 --> 00:35:04,300
And ideally, that overlap has some buffer

711
00:35:04,300 --> 00:35:08,340
Because if, for example, when you're trying to find the pasta

712
00:35:08,340 --> 00:35:10,660
it only works if you're standing exactly

713
00:35:10,660 --> 00:35:12,660
in front of the boiled water and not if you're

714
00:35:12,660 --> 00:35:14,140
like a little bit to the right

715
00:35:14,140 --> 00:35:17,150
Then if your policy ends up deciding to boil the water

716
00:35:17,150 --> 00:35:18,860
standing a little bit to the right of it

717
00:35:18,860 --> 00:35:22,640
then you'll get a brittle final result where

718
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:30,320
it will struggle in that case

719
00:35:30,320 --> 00:35:32,080
I guess one way to think about it

720
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:34,880
is that if this low-level policy only

721
00:35:34,880 --> 00:35:39,640
works from this part of the states

722
00:35:39,640 --> 00:35:42,800
where the water is being boiled and the high-level policy gets

723
00:35:42,800 --> 00:35:45,500
you here, or the previous low-level policy gets you here

724
00:35:45,500 --> 00:35:48,000
then you're going to have a little bit of a gap between here

725
00:35:48,000 --> 00:35:48,680
and here

726
00:35:48,680 --> 00:35:51,040
And that sort of gap can lead to brittle performance

727
00:35:51,040 --> 00:35:53,580
on the full long-horizon task

728
00:35:53,580 --> 00:35:54,080
Yeah

729
00:35:54,080 --> 00:35:56,460
So the question is, does it have to be a fixed sequence

730
00:35:56,460 --> 00:35:58,520
And can you learn if one sequence

731
00:35:58,520 --> 00:36:00,920
is more efficient than another

732
00:36:00,920 --> 00:36:01,420
Yeah

733
00:36:01,420 --> 00:36:03,160
So absolutely, the high-level policy

734
00:36:03,160 --> 00:36:05,280
can actually decide which sequence it wants to do

735
00:36:05,280 --> 00:36:07,780
and that sequence might depend on what's the most efficient

736
00:36:07,780 --> 00:36:13,920
It might also depend on where the low-level policy is

737
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:14,990
most successful

738
00:36:14,990 --> 00:36:18,010
And so one of the reasons why I wanted to pick this example

739
00:36:18,010 --> 00:36:20,830
is actually these two steps can be done in any order

740
00:36:20,830 --> 00:36:23,470
And the high-level policy could decide that when you're here

741
00:36:23,470 --> 00:36:25,730
it actually wants to command you to go here

742
00:36:25,730 --> 00:36:27,250
And as long as the low-level policy

743
00:36:27,250 --> 00:36:29,970
knows how to find the pasta from these states

744
00:36:29,970 --> 00:36:32,900
then that might be more efficient overall

745
00:36:32,900 --> 00:36:34,650
There's also worlds where maybe this would

746
00:36:34,650 --> 00:36:36,510
be more efficient because maybe while you're boiling the water

747
00:36:36,510 --> 00:36:37,340
you're just waiting

748
00:36:37,340 --> 00:36:41,150
And you can find the pasta in the meantime

749
00:36:41,150 --> 00:36:43,730
And so I guess there's also a pouring water that-- and turning

750
00:36:43,730 --> 00:36:44,810
on the stove and so forth

751
00:36:44,810 --> 00:36:46,150
So there's more steps involved

752
00:36:46,150 --> 00:36:48,090
But yeah, the high-level policy can decide

753
00:36:48,090 --> 00:36:52,230
which order it wants to take

754
00:36:52,230 --> 00:36:55,450
So generally the way to address this

755
00:36:55,450 --> 00:36:57,970
is that you can first train them potentially completely

756
00:36:57,970 --> 00:36:59,130
separately

757
00:36:59,130 --> 00:37:01,730
But it's important for at least one policy

758
00:37:01,730 --> 00:37:05,450
to be adapted to the other

759
00:37:05,450 --> 00:37:10,810
And ideally, even both could be jointly adapted with each other

760
00:37:10,810 --> 00:37:18,590
to optimize reward, or optimize some complete objective

761
00:37:18,590 --> 00:37:19,590
Cool

762
00:37:19,590 --> 00:37:22,750
One other side note, I guess, that I

763
00:37:22,750 --> 00:37:26,130
think is worth mentioning is that the--

764
00:37:26,130 --> 00:37:31,430
even for problems that are not traditionally LLM problems--

765
00:37:31,430 --> 00:37:34,110
LLMs are actually often pretty good at some

766
00:37:34,110 --> 00:37:37,430
of the higher-level reasoning that you might want to do

767
00:37:37,430 --> 00:37:40,540
And so, for example, if you know that you want to cook pasta

768
00:37:40,540 --> 00:37:42,830
if you ask a language model what are the steps involved

769
00:37:42,830 --> 00:37:45,790
with that, oftentimes, they can actually give you that

770
00:37:45,790 --> 00:37:48,950
And so this is one way where you can actually

771
00:37:48,950 --> 00:37:51,470
train the high-level policy completely separately

772
00:37:51,470 --> 00:37:54,700
from the low-level policy and then potentially fine-tune them

773
00:37:54,700 --> 00:37:56,370
because the language model, for example

774
00:37:56,370 --> 00:38:02,590
might not know which order the low-level policy is best suited

775
00:38:02,590 --> 00:38:06,590
for, or other things about the capabilities

776
00:38:06,590 --> 00:38:09,220
of the low-level policy

777
00:38:09,220 --> 00:38:10,850
And so that sort of adaptation process

778
00:38:10,850 --> 00:38:12,380
even though language models are pretty good at this

779
00:38:12,380 --> 00:38:15,000
that sort of adaptation process is really important

780
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:18,280
Also, if a robot is in front of a coffee machine

781
00:38:18,280 --> 00:38:20,510
and you ask the robot to get some coffee for you

782
00:38:20,510 --> 00:38:22,680
if the language model doesn't actually pay attention

783
00:38:22,680 --> 00:38:25,860
to that image, it might tell the robot like, oh

784
00:38:25,860 --> 00:38:29,490
can you go out and buy coffee at Starbucks or something

785
00:38:29,490 --> 00:38:31,700
And the robot might not have that sort of affordance

786
00:38:31,700 --> 00:38:33,360
that sort of ability to do that

787
00:38:33,360 --> 00:38:35,320
And so adapting these two together

788
00:38:35,320 --> 00:38:37,280
that's another example where adapting these two

789
00:38:37,280 --> 00:38:38,660
high-level and low-level policies

790
00:38:38,660 --> 00:38:43,380
together is quite important

791
00:38:43,380 --> 00:38:47,920
The last key design choice that we haven't talked about yet

792
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:51,280
is when to move from one task to another

793
00:38:51,280 --> 00:38:55,240
So we outlined this process here

794
00:38:55,240 --> 00:38:57,860
and we didn't really spend that much time thinking about

795
00:38:57,860 --> 00:39:01,200
like, how long do you repeat sampling

796
00:39:01,200 --> 00:39:04,760
from your low-level policy to accomplish a goal

797
00:39:04,760 --> 00:39:06,760
And essentially, it's how often do we

798
00:39:06,760 --> 00:39:09,560
requery a new goal from the high-level policy

799
00:39:09,560 --> 00:39:13,290
to pass through the low-level policy

800
00:39:13,290 --> 00:39:18,170
So basically, yeah, this step right here

801
00:39:18,170 --> 00:39:24,330
So there's two natural options for this

802
00:39:24,330 --> 00:39:29,730
The first natural option is to requery the high-level policy

803
00:39:29,730 --> 00:39:33,110
whenever the low-level policy has completed the goal

804
00:39:33,110 --> 00:39:35,810
So when it has boiled the water, that's

805
00:39:35,810 --> 00:39:40,890
when you tell it to go find the pasta, for example

806
00:39:40,890 --> 00:39:43,090
And the way that this would look like is--

807
00:39:43,090 --> 00:39:45,210
at this point, your low-level policy not only

808
00:39:45,210 --> 00:39:49,290
needs to predict action but also needs to predict whether

809
00:39:49,290 --> 00:39:53,610
or it's successfully completed the task

810
00:39:53,610 --> 00:39:57,290
And this could be either with some sort of binary indicator

811
00:39:57,290 --> 00:40:01,050
or through some sort of progress indicator

812
00:40:01,050 --> 00:40:03,490
You can think about this not done as a success

813
00:40:03,490 --> 00:40:08,330
classifier for the task

814
00:40:08,330 --> 00:40:14,390
And then the other option that's a bit simpler but maybe less

815
00:40:14,390 --> 00:40:17,550
elegant, in some ways, is just to simply requery

816
00:40:17,550 --> 00:40:21,230
the high-level policy after some fixed number of timesteps

817
00:40:21,230 --> 00:40:27,490
So maybe you query the low-level policy at 50 hertz

818
00:40:27,490 --> 00:40:31,590
and you query the high-level policy once every second

819
00:40:31,590 --> 00:40:34,350
So then you would be querying the high-level policy

820
00:40:34,350 --> 00:40:38,430
after 50 timesteps

821
00:40:38,430 --> 00:40:42,710
So there's pros and cons of each of these two options

822
00:40:42,710 --> 00:40:45,590
For the first case where we're doing something

823
00:40:45,590 --> 00:40:48,860
after we've completed a goal, this is actually ideal

824
00:40:48,860 --> 00:40:50,230
If you were to do this well, this

825
00:40:50,230 --> 00:40:52,390
should be the most compute efficient

826
00:40:52,390 --> 00:40:55,070
because you're waiting for the low-level policy

827
00:40:55,070 --> 00:40:56,910
You don't need to rethink how you're

828
00:40:56,910 --> 00:41:02,490
doing the task while the low-level policy is executing

829
00:41:02,490 --> 00:41:05,030
There's a couple of things that are tricky about this though

830
00:41:05,030 --> 00:41:06,670
The first is that it can actually

831
00:41:06,670 --> 00:41:11,400
be hard to estimate when you're done with a task

832
00:41:11,400 --> 00:41:14,700
It's definitely not trivial to do this

833
00:41:14,700 --> 00:41:16,160
And the second is that you actually

834
00:41:16,160 --> 00:41:20,000
need to account for mistakes that require actually

835
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:22,560
redoing some past goal

836
00:41:22,560 --> 00:41:26,080
So for example, if you are trying

837
00:41:26,080 --> 00:41:29,320
to organize some things on a table, you go grab something

838
00:41:29,320 --> 00:41:31,600
and then you accidentally drop it on the ground

839
00:41:31,600 --> 00:41:34,160
Your subgoal should no longer be like, put that object

840
00:41:34,160 --> 00:41:36,080
on the table, and instead it should be

841
00:41:36,080 --> 00:41:38,100
to pick it up off the ground

842
00:41:38,100 --> 00:41:39,640
And this means that actually you need

843
00:41:39,640 --> 00:41:42,040
to now estimate not just when you're

844
00:41:42,040 --> 00:41:47,800
done but also whether or not you've actually

845
00:41:47,800 --> 00:41:55,240
exited the states in which that subtask makes sense

846
00:41:55,240 --> 00:41:59,240
Then going back to the fixed number of timesteps

847
00:41:59,240 --> 00:42:03,160
this is less ideal because we're going to be probably wasting

848
00:42:03,160 --> 00:42:05,480
some compute, and there's going to be

849
00:42:05,480 --> 00:42:08,120
a trade-off between spending more compute

850
00:42:08,120 --> 00:42:11,860
thinking through what the next goal should be

851
00:42:11,860 --> 00:42:14,380
versus if this n is too long

852
00:42:14,380 --> 00:42:16,820
Then you're going to be delayed in transitioning

853
00:42:16,820 --> 00:42:20,620
to the new goal, where your agent will be-- for example

854
00:42:20,620 --> 00:42:22,480
maybe it's found the pot

855
00:42:22,480 --> 00:42:24,970
It's taken out the pot, and it's waiting for a new goal

856
00:42:24,970 --> 00:42:26,340
If n is too long, it's just going

857
00:42:26,340 --> 00:42:28,630
to be sitting there with the pot because it's like, OK

858
00:42:28,630 --> 00:42:29,460
I found the pot

859
00:42:29,460 --> 00:42:31,070
And that's the goal that it's getting

860
00:42:31,070 --> 00:42:32,780
And there'll be some delay before it then

861
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:34,700
moves on to actually finding the pasta

862
00:42:34,700 --> 00:42:37,220
or pouring water into the pot

863
00:42:37,220 --> 00:42:40,780
So this trade-off between how long n should be

864
00:42:40,780 --> 00:42:44,320
Generally, because you don't want these really long delays

865
00:42:44,320 --> 00:42:49,660
you'll pick n to be fairly small or fairly frequent

866
00:42:49,660 --> 00:42:52,140
But when n is smaller, you're then

867
00:42:52,140 --> 00:42:55,060
going to be placing more burden on your high-level policy

868
00:42:55,060 --> 00:42:59,020
because it needs to be making accurate goal predictions more

869
00:42:59,020 --> 00:43:02,220
frequently on more states

870
00:43:02,220 --> 00:43:06,510
And you'll be spending more compute as well

871
00:43:06,510 --> 00:43:10,870
So both of these have pros and cons

872
00:43:10,870 --> 00:43:17,670
In general, if we wanted to think about how these pros

873
00:43:17,670 --> 00:43:19,190
and cons relate to each other, we

874
00:43:19,190 --> 00:43:21,230
can think about how these pros and cons lead

875
00:43:21,230 --> 00:43:24,070
to different downstream performance

876
00:43:24,070 --> 00:43:28,110
And with regard to estimating when you're done

877
00:43:28,110 --> 00:43:30,230
and accounting for mistakes, if you actually

878
00:43:30,230 --> 00:43:33,840
make a mistake in estimating when you're done, then

879
00:43:33,840 --> 00:43:35,510
you could actually end up in a situation

880
00:43:35,510 --> 00:43:39,140
where the agent is perpetually stuck

881
00:43:39,140 --> 00:43:41,950
If it thinks that it's not done but it is actually done

882
00:43:41,950 --> 00:43:44,510
then it will actually just stay in that state

883
00:43:44,510 --> 00:43:48,110
because the low-level policy will keep on predicting this--

884
00:43:48,110 --> 00:43:50,430
won't actually be making any more further progress

885
00:43:50,430 --> 00:43:52,550
on the task

886
00:43:52,550 --> 00:43:56,710
So this actually is a pretty rough outcome

887
00:43:56,710 --> 00:44:01,470
for these kinds of errors, even if they're infrequent

888
00:44:01,470 --> 00:44:04,530
And then for option two, if you make a mistake

889
00:44:04,530 --> 00:44:06,370
in predicting the subtask because you're

890
00:44:06,370 --> 00:44:08,890
querying the high-level policy more frequently

891
00:44:08,890 --> 00:44:11,490
this also has bad outcome, which is that the low-level policy

892
00:44:11,490 --> 00:44:14,830
might stop making progress on the final task

893
00:44:14,830 --> 00:44:17,970
and might be taking the wrong actions for n steps

894
00:44:17,970 --> 00:44:22,650
until you get hopefully a more accurate goal prediction

895
00:44:22,650 --> 00:44:24,850
And generally, between these two things

896
00:44:24,850 --> 00:44:28,130
these errors are more fatal

897
00:44:28,130 --> 00:44:32,610
And as a result, typically, a lot of the hierarchical systems

898
00:44:32,610 --> 00:44:36,010
out there opt for choosing option two because

899
00:44:36,010 --> 00:44:38,320
of the simplicity and because some

900
00:44:38,320 --> 00:44:40,530
of the mistakes that the high-level policy might make

901
00:44:40,530 --> 00:44:42,170
when it's queried more frequently

902
00:44:42,170 --> 00:44:44,450
are less fatal than mistakes with regard

903
00:44:44,450 --> 00:44:47,650
to estimating when you're done and at what subtask

904
00:44:47,650 --> 00:44:50,240
whether or not you should re-account for past goals

905
00:44:50,240 --> 00:44:52,450
It's also worth mentioning that you could potentially

906
00:44:52,450 --> 00:44:55,350
combine these, where you try to estimate progress

907
00:44:55,350 --> 00:44:57,430
But then if it's been a long period of time

908
00:44:57,430 --> 00:44:59,610
then you're re-query anyway

909
00:44:59,610 --> 00:45:02,010
But yeah, the takeaway is that, generally, option two

910
00:45:02,010 --> 00:45:04,500
is typically what people do, where you just query

911
00:45:04,500 --> 00:45:07,860
at a fixed lower frequency

912
00:45:07,860 --> 00:45:08,760
Cool

913
00:45:08,760 --> 00:45:11,500
So let's look at some example systems

914
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:14,140
And I guess one thing that I'll mention

915
00:45:14,140 --> 00:45:17,320
is that hierarchy is pretty popular these days

916
00:45:17,320 --> 00:45:23,660
So here are four systems that we're all

917
00:45:23,660 --> 00:45:27,340
announced in the last five months, four months

918
00:45:27,340 --> 00:45:29,560
And they actually all use hierarchical systems

919
00:45:29,560 --> 00:45:31,620
So these are all fairly long horizon tasks

920
00:45:31,620 --> 00:45:35,060
where you're cleaning a bedroom, handing over objects

921
00:45:35,060 --> 00:45:37,860
and pouring them or putting them into bins

922
00:45:37,860 --> 00:45:40,320
putting away objects into a fridge

923
00:45:40,320 --> 00:45:43,300
putting fruit into bowls

924
00:45:43,300 --> 00:45:46,100
And if you look at the architectures for these things

925
00:45:46,100 --> 00:45:50,220
they all have some higher-level model

926
00:45:50,220 --> 00:45:52,020
that's predicting intermediate goals

927
00:45:52,020 --> 00:45:57,740
and some lower-level model that is predicting low-level actions

928
00:45:57,740 --> 00:45:59,120
These are in robotics domains

929
00:45:59,120 --> 00:46:01,750
Likewise in language model domains

930
00:46:01,750 --> 00:46:03,920
things like chain of thought, where you're reasoning

931
00:46:03,920 --> 00:46:05,120
through intermediate steps

932
00:46:05,120 --> 00:46:07,680
And then actually outputting tokens for those steps

933
00:46:07,680 --> 00:46:12,400
is also really popular for completing more challenging

934
00:46:12,400 --> 00:46:16,480
language tasks

935
00:46:16,480 --> 00:46:17,580
Cool

936
00:46:17,580 --> 00:46:20,720
So I want to cover a few examples of these systems

937
00:46:20,720 --> 00:46:23,560
and I picked examples that I think

938
00:46:23,560 --> 00:46:26,440
illustrate a few different design choices that are helpful

939
00:46:26,440 --> 00:46:29,360
and actually have comparisons on those design choices

940
00:46:29,360 --> 00:46:32,400
that elucidate some of the benefits of one design

941
00:46:32,400 --> 00:46:34,790
choice versus another

942
00:46:34,790 --> 00:46:37,120
So let's start with talking about hierarchical imitation

943
00:46:37,120 --> 00:46:38,950
learning systems

944
00:46:38,950 --> 00:46:45,280
And we'll start with language subgoals

945
00:46:45,280 --> 00:46:47,400
And let's first talk about what the data would look

946
00:46:47,400 --> 00:46:49,600
like for training a hierarchical imitation learning system

947
00:46:49,600 --> 00:46:55,160
with language subgoals

948
00:46:55,160 --> 00:46:58,150
This will be kind of a real robot example

949
00:46:58,150 --> 00:46:59,720
What the data looks like is actually

950
00:46:59,720 --> 00:47:03,650
it's going to use data of a long-horizon demonstration

951
00:47:03,650 --> 00:47:07,010
of completing the complete long-horizon task

952
00:47:07,010 --> 00:47:09,610
And so in this case, the task is to make trail mix

953
00:47:09,610 --> 00:47:12,210
and put the trail mix into a bag

954
00:47:12,210 --> 00:47:16,770
And with this long-horizon demonstration for the full task

955
00:47:16,770 --> 00:47:20,890
it will then be segmented with language labels

956
00:47:20,890 --> 00:47:22,890
for each segment

957
00:47:22,890 --> 00:47:26,490
And in this case, the segments in the data

958
00:47:26,490 --> 00:47:32,690
can have a variable length

959
00:47:32,690 --> 00:47:37,450
If, for example, the robot trajectory

960
00:47:37,450 --> 00:47:39,590
looks something like this, then you might have--

961
00:47:39,590 --> 00:47:42,270
the first subtask is something like this

962
00:47:42,270 --> 00:47:44,450
The second subtask is maybe shorter

963
00:47:44,450 --> 00:47:46,430
The third subtask is maybe longer

964
00:47:46,430 --> 00:47:49,250
So you'll have variable frequency in the data

965
00:47:49,250 --> 00:47:53,450
but the actual system will later be using a fixed frequency where

966
00:47:53,450 --> 00:47:59,340
it's requiring more frequently than the typical segment

967
00:47:59,340 --> 00:48:03,020
length in the data

968
00:48:03,020 --> 00:48:05,100
So these are different language labels

969
00:48:05,100 --> 00:48:10,400
These labels could either be recorded during data collection

970
00:48:10,400 --> 00:48:12,820
where you're actually saying, I'm going to pick up the bag

971
00:48:12,820 --> 00:48:14,720
and then do a demonstration of picking up the bag

972
00:48:14,720 --> 00:48:16,680
Then say, I'm going to pick up the metal scoop

973
00:48:16,680 --> 00:48:18,990
do a demonstration of that in sequence

974
00:48:18,990 --> 00:48:20,660
or you can do it post-hoc where you just

975
00:48:20,660 --> 00:48:22,480
collect the full demonstration

976
00:48:22,480 --> 00:48:25,060
And then with a labeling interface label

977
00:48:25,060 --> 00:48:26,810
like, this segment is picking up the bag

978
00:48:26,810 --> 00:48:28,600
This segment is picking up the metal scoop

979
00:48:28,600 --> 00:48:33,600
This segment is getting M&Ms and so forth

980
00:48:33,600 --> 00:48:35,060
So we have segmented demonstrations

981
00:48:35,060 --> 00:48:37,220
with the associated language command

982
00:48:37,220 --> 00:48:40,140
Here's another example of what this would look like

983
00:48:40,140 --> 00:48:43,300
And this shows that one segment might be longer for picking up

984
00:48:43,300 --> 00:48:45,380
a slice of cheese, a different segment

985
00:48:45,380 --> 00:48:47,660
might be shorter for putting the cheese on the bread

986
00:48:47,660 --> 00:48:51,360
and so forth

987
00:48:51,360 --> 00:48:51,860
Cool

988
00:48:51,860 --> 00:48:54,460
And then once we have this data, we'll

989
00:48:54,460 --> 00:48:57,020
train a high-level policy and a low-level policy

990
00:48:57,020 --> 00:48:58,640
with imitation learning

991
00:48:58,640 --> 00:49:01,640
And the high-level policy will be outputting the language

992
00:49:01,640 --> 00:49:04,600
labels from the observations

993
00:49:04,600 --> 00:49:08,400
And the low level will be taking the language

994
00:49:08,400 --> 00:49:13,320
labels and the observations and predicting the actions

995
00:49:13,320 --> 00:49:17,940
So what this looks like is an image observation

996
00:49:17,940 --> 00:49:20,840
So an RGB image passes it into a high-level policy

997
00:49:20,840 --> 00:49:24,360
that's trained to predict the language label

998
00:49:24,360 --> 00:49:27,860
And then you'll have a low-level policy that takes the language

999
00:49:27,860 --> 00:49:30,140
in addition to the observations

1000
00:49:30,140 --> 00:49:34,780
In this case, the work chooses to use not just RGB images

1001
00:49:34,780 --> 00:49:38,280
but also joint angles as input to the low-level policy

1002
00:49:38,280 --> 00:49:40,320
And generally, it's OK to have actually

1003
00:49:40,320 --> 00:49:43,120
this sort of mismatch between observations for the high-level

1004
00:49:43,120 --> 00:49:45,040
and the low-level policy

1005
00:49:45,040 --> 00:49:49,840
And then the low-level policy outputs joint commands

1006
00:49:49,840 --> 00:49:52,840
And the low-level policy operates at 50 hertz

1007
00:49:52,840 --> 00:49:54,520
And I can't remember the exact frequency

1008
00:49:54,520 --> 00:49:56,060
of the high-level policy, but I think

1009
00:49:56,060 --> 00:50:03,130
it's something around 1 hertz

1010
00:50:03,130 --> 00:50:05,110
Cool

1011
00:50:05,110 --> 00:50:07,130
So that's the general setup

1012
00:50:07,130 --> 00:50:09,850
Now we also learned about DAgger

1013
00:50:09,850 --> 00:50:13,090
And you can think about if we just

1014
00:50:13,090 --> 00:50:15,370
do imitation learning on the offline data

1015
00:50:15,370 --> 00:50:18,210
we might have issues where we're training these

1016
00:50:18,210 --> 00:50:20,810
somewhat independently, although the data is at least

1017
00:50:20,810 --> 00:50:23,810
of the full long-horizon task

1018
00:50:23,810 --> 00:50:29,610
And we could actually use DAgger with these systems

1019
00:50:29,610 --> 00:50:33,550
on both the low-level policy and the high-level policy

1020
00:50:33,550 --> 00:50:34,930
Now, one thing that's pretty cool

1021
00:50:34,930 --> 00:50:36,720
is that actually for the high-level policy

1022
00:50:36,720 --> 00:50:38,770
we can actually update the high-level policy

1023
00:50:38,770 --> 00:50:40,850
with language supervision

1024
00:50:40,850 --> 00:50:43,090
And so DAgger on the high-level policy

1025
00:50:43,090 --> 00:50:48,930
is actually in language space, not in joint space

1026
00:50:48,930 --> 00:50:55,310
And so in principle, you can do DAgger on both low level

1027
00:50:55,310 --> 00:50:55,970
and high level

1028
00:50:55,970 --> 00:50:57,430
In this particular approach, if you

1029
00:50:57,430 --> 00:51:00,150
were to do it on the high level with freezing the low level

1030
00:51:00,150 --> 00:51:05,430
you would be running the policy, the full system

1031
00:51:05,430 --> 00:51:09,110
And then you would then type or speak

1032
00:51:09,110 --> 00:51:11,670
a language command that is going to override

1033
00:51:11,670 --> 00:51:17,740
the high-level policy and then continue collecting data

1034
00:51:17,740 --> 00:51:19,870
And then at some point, you might give control back

1035
00:51:19,870 --> 00:51:23,070
to the high-level policy

1036
00:51:23,070 --> 00:51:25,190
And then from there, you can fine-tune and actually

1037
00:51:25,190 --> 00:51:28,510
do gradient steps on data that has these kinds of language

1038
00:51:28,510 --> 00:51:32,270
interventions to actually fine-tune the high-level policy

1039
00:51:32,270 --> 00:51:35,870
And when you do this together with the high-level policy

1040
00:51:35,870 --> 00:51:40,170
and the low-level policy acting as one system

1041
00:51:40,170 --> 00:51:42,470
then you can actually adapt the high-level policy

1042
00:51:42,470 --> 00:51:44,990
to deficiencies in the low-level policy

1043
00:51:44,990 --> 00:51:46,990
So, for example, if there's one language command

1044
00:51:46,990 --> 00:51:50,150
that the low-level policy makes a mistake on

1045
00:51:50,150 --> 00:51:54,800
then you could actually issue some corrections to help

1046
00:51:54,800 --> 00:52:00,560
compensate for that mistake

1047
00:52:00,560 --> 00:52:01,220
So yeah

1048
00:52:01,220 --> 00:52:04,240
I guess to summarize, we can train the high-level policy

1049
00:52:04,240 --> 00:52:06,440
and the low-level policy, both with imitation

1050
00:52:06,440 --> 00:52:08,320
and then freeze the low-level policy

1051
00:52:08,320 --> 00:52:11,320
and update the high level policy with DAgger by supervising

1052
00:52:11,320 --> 00:52:15,080
on language corrections

1053
00:52:15,080 --> 00:52:17,280
And so as a video of what this looks like

1054
00:52:17,280 --> 00:52:21,000
these yellow commands are showing the output

1055
00:52:21,000 --> 00:52:22,920
of the high-level policy

1056
00:52:22,920 --> 00:52:25,050
And then the robot motions are showing the output

1057
00:52:25,050 --> 00:52:30,040
of the low-level policy

1058
00:52:30,040 --> 00:52:33,360
So at the beginning, the high-level policy is saying

1059
00:52:33,360 --> 00:52:34,020
pick up spoon

1060
00:52:34,020 --> 00:52:36,560
The robot picks up the spoon, move the spoon towards me

1061
00:52:36,560 --> 00:52:38,060
So then it says I want some peanuts

1062
00:52:38,060 --> 00:52:39,920
So then the low-level policy scoops peanuts

1063
00:52:39,920 --> 00:52:42,420
Then the high-level policy says move the scoop into the bag

1064
00:52:42,420 --> 00:52:44,050
The low-level policy starts to do that

1065
00:52:44,050 --> 00:52:46,210
At this point, you'll actually notice that the robot

1066
00:52:46,210 --> 00:52:47,520
is about to make a mistake

1067
00:52:47,520 --> 00:52:49,560
And so that's when you can intervene

1068
00:52:49,560 --> 00:52:52,890
And instead of telling the robot move the scoop in the bag

1069
00:52:52,890 --> 00:52:55,140
because it seems like the low-level policy isn't going

1070
00:52:55,140 --> 00:53:01,600
to do that correctly, you could then intervene and say

1071
00:53:01,600 --> 00:53:02,840
move the scoop into the bag

1072
00:53:02,840 --> 00:53:04,340
Or instead of saying move the scoop

1073
00:53:04,340 --> 00:53:07,020
you say move the left arm to the left

1074
00:53:07,020 --> 00:53:10,720
go higher, move the scoop into the bag, then

1075
00:53:10,720 --> 00:53:13,220
And so these user commands are overriding

1076
00:53:13,220 --> 00:53:15,100
the high-level policy

1077
00:53:15,100 --> 00:53:18,180
And then at this point, you can give control back

1078
00:53:18,180 --> 00:53:19,600
to the high-level policy

1079
00:53:19,600 --> 00:53:27,020
And the high-level policy predicts, pour into the bag

1080
00:53:27,020 --> 00:53:29,900
get some banana chips, and so forth

1081
00:53:29,900 --> 00:53:31,860
So that's what this sort of DAgger intervention

1082
00:53:31,860 --> 00:53:34,340
looks in the high-level space

1083
00:53:34,340 --> 00:53:36,500
And then after you fine-tune the high-level policy

1084
00:53:36,500 --> 00:53:39,220
on those kinds of interventions, the high-level policy

1085
00:53:39,220 --> 00:53:41,940
can then self-correct and know that the low-level policy

1086
00:53:41,940 --> 00:53:43,780
is making a mistake, where it was

1087
00:53:43,780 --> 00:53:46,100
about to make the same sort of mistake again

1088
00:53:46,100 --> 00:53:49,700
And then the high-level policy says, go higher, and then

1089
00:53:49,700 --> 00:53:53,400
move the scoop into the bag to prevent that sort of mistake

1090
00:53:53,400 --> 00:53:56,070
And so this is an example of the high-level and low-level policy

1091
00:53:56,070 --> 00:54:02,070
adapting together to account for deficiencies

1092
00:54:02,070 --> 00:54:03,870
Cool

1093
00:54:03,870 --> 00:54:06,310
Now we've talked about hierarchy versus flat policies

1094
00:54:06,310 --> 00:54:06,910
You could ask

1095
00:54:06,910 --> 00:54:08,950
You can actually do a comparison

1096
00:54:08,950 --> 00:54:12,710
In this particular setup, does hierarchy

1097
00:54:12,710 --> 00:54:14,870
outperform using a single flat policy

1098
00:54:14,870 --> 00:54:18,550
to do the long-horizon task

1099
00:54:18,550 --> 00:54:21,130
This comparison is actually from a different research paper

1100
00:54:21,130 --> 00:54:25,110
but it has basically the same approach

1101
00:54:25,110 --> 00:54:26,830
In terms of task progress, there's

1102
00:54:26,830 --> 00:54:29,070
a 34% improvement from using hierarchy

1103
00:54:29,070 --> 00:54:31,110
versus training a single policy to do

1104
00:54:31,110 --> 00:54:34,990
the full long-horizon imitation learning task

1105
00:54:34,990 --> 00:54:36,730
And in terms of following instructions

1106
00:54:36,730 --> 00:54:38,290
there's also a gap

1107
00:54:38,290 --> 00:54:42,110
This is this instruction accuracy metric

1108
00:54:42,110 --> 00:54:44,390
And you can also actually compare

1109
00:54:44,390 --> 00:54:48,820
DAgger to imitation learning for the high-level policy

1110
00:54:48,820 --> 00:54:50,200
And you can also see that there's

1111
00:54:50,200 --> 00:54:52,200
an improvement from using DAgger compared to just using

1112
00:54:52,200 --> 00:54:53,380
vanilla imitation learning

1113
00:54:53,380 --> 00:54:54,880
And this is the improvement that you

1114
00:54:54,880 --> 00:55:00,320
get from adopting the policies together

1115
00:55:00,320 --> 00:55:03,070
Cool

1116
00:55:03,070 --> 00:55:04,500
The nice thing about hierarchies

1117
00:55:04,500 --> 00:55:05,940
you can do longer horizon tasks

1118
00:55:05,940 --> 00:55:10,760
And so with this full system, you can, for example

1119
00:55:10,760 --> 00:55:14,320
get the robot to put three objects into a Ziploc bag, which

1120
00:55:14,320 --> 00:55:16,560
is traditionally a pretty difficult task

1121
00:55:16,560 --> 00:55:20,840
to do, especially in one go

1122
00:55:20,840 --> 00:55:22,500
And likewise with a similar system

1123
00:55:22,500 --> 00:55:25,160
you can also get a robot to--

1124
00:55:25,160 --> 00:55:27,260
the high-level command is clean the bedroom

1125
00:55:27,260 --> 00:55:28,680
And then the low-level policy will

1126
00:55:28,680 --> 00:55:31,660
output things like pick up shirt, put shirt in basket

1127
00:55:31,660 --> 00:55:34,640
pick up shirt, put shirt in basket

1128
00:55:34,640 --> 00:55:39,360
And later pick up the pillow, adjust the blanket

1129
00:55:39,360 --> 00:55:42,040
put the pillow at the top of the bed, adjust the pillow

1130
00:55:42,040 --> 00:55:45,520
pick up tissue, put the tissue in the trash can, and so forth

1131
00:55:45,520 --> 00:55:47,100
And so these are things that are like

1132
00:55:47,100 --> 00:55:52,230
would be very hard to do if you just train a single flat policy

1133
00:55:52,230 --> 00:55:54,020
So instead of using language, we could also

1134
00:55:54,020 --> 00:55:57,790
use images as that intermediate representation

1135
00:55:57,790 --> 00:55:59,580
And so what this would look like is instead

1136
00:55:59,580 --> 00:56:01,940
of having the high-level policy output language

1137
00:56:01,940 --> 00:56:03,880
you would output a goal image

1138
00:56:03,880 --> 00:56:05,420
And so it'd be like, this is the kind

1139
00:56:05,420 --> 00:56:08,910
of an image of what I want you to accomplish in the future

1140
00:56:08,910 --> 00:56:10,740
In practice, you can use a generative model

1141
00:56:10,740 --> 00:56:12,540
like an image-editing model to do

1142
00:56:12,540 --> 00:56:15,060
this, that takes as input the image of the current step

1143
00:56:15,060 --> 00:56:18,250
and then edits that to look like an image at a future timestep

1144
00:56:18,250 --> 00:56:22,220
that you want the robot to achieve

1145
00:56:22,220 --> 00:56:24,100
And then the low-level policy would no longer

1146
00:56:24,100 --> 00:56:25,940
be language conditioned but would rather

1147
00:56:25,940 --> 00:56:28,460
be a goal image conditioned policy

1148
00:56:28,460 --> 00:56:31,940
And so you can think of this as a kind of a multi-step inverse

1149
00:56:31,940 --> 00:56:35,060
model that takes as input the current image

1150
00:56:35,060 --> 00:56:37,300
some future image, and then outputs the actions

1151
00:56:37,300 --> 00:56:41,020
that you can take to get to that goal image

1152
00:56:41,020 --> 00:56:43,660
And then both of these will be trained again

1153
00:56:43,660 --> 00:56:45,620
with imitation learning

1154
00:56:45,620 --> 00:56:47,870
And one of the things that's pretty nice

1155
00:56:47,870 --> 00:56:50,790
is that you no longer need to segment videos with language

1156
00:56:50,790 --> 00:56:52,590
annotations to do this

1157
00:56:52,590 --> 00:56:55,870
You just need the videos of the long-horizon task

1158
00:56:55,870 --> 00:56:58,330
And to train this high-level policy

1159
00:56:58,330 --> 00:57:02,110
you can train it on robot data and predict future images

1160
00:57:02,110 --> 00:57:04,910
in the trajectory, like from 1 second or 5 seconds

1161
00:57:04,910 --> 00:57:08,060
into the future, or 10 seconds in the future

1162
00:57:08,060 --> 00:57:10,390
But you could also actually train this high-level policy

1163
00:57:10,390 --> 00:57:14,070
using YouTube videos, or something like that

1164
00:57:14,070 --> 00:57:16,830
to predict what are future images that might accomplish

1165
00:57:16,830 --> 00:57:19,670
some long-term goal

1166
00:57:19,670 --> 00:57:22,550
And that sort of unlabeled video data

1167
00:57:22,550 --> 00:57:25,310
can be helpful, especially if data from the robot

1168
00:57:25,310 --> 00:57:29,830
is more sparse

1169
00:57:29,830 --> 00:57:31,990
So then an example of what this looks like

1170
00:57:31,990 --> 00:57:36,150
is, say, the task is to move the wooden bowl

1171
00:57:36,150 --> 00:57:37,630
to the top of the table

1172
00:57:37,630 --> 00:57:40,150
You pass that into the high-level policy that

1173
00:57:40,150 --> 00:57:44,110
then generates images, which are shown with the pink border

1174
00:57:44,110 --> 00:57:47,610
and then that generated subgoal image with the pink border

1175
00:57:47,610 --> 00:57:50,130
is passed as input to the low-level policy, which

1176
00:57:50,130 --> 00:57:51,750
then predicts actions

1177
00:57:51,750 --> 00:57:53,250
And both of these policies again are

1178
00:57:53,250 --> 00:57:57,410
trained with imitation learning on demonstration data

1179
00:57:57,410 --> 00:58:00,090
and potentially also if for the high-level policy

1180
00:58:00,090 --> 00:58:03,260
can be trained on video data as well

1181
00:58:03,260 --> 00:58:05,630
So this one is putting the toothpaste in the wooden bowl

1182
00:58:05,630 --> 00:58:07,880
You can see that it generates these subgoal images of

1183
00:58:07,880 --> 00:58:10,930
for example, holding the toothpaste above the bowl

1184
00:58:10,930 --> 00:58:12,190
It then reaches that

1185
00:58:12,190 --> 00:58:15,310
Then it generates a subgoal of lowering the arm

1186
00:58:15,310 --> 00:58:20,730
And so the robot lowers the arm to complete the task

1187
00:58:20,730 --> 00:58:23,190
And then this last example, the goal is to open the drawer

1188
00:58:23,190 --> 00:58:26,970
The first subgoal is to put the gripper on the handle

1189
00:58:26,970 --> 00:58:30,890
Then the next subgoal is to move the gripper to the right

1190
00:58:30,890 --> 00:58:34,360
so that it opens, and so forth

1191
00:58:34,360 --> 00:58:36,570
Another thing that's nice about this sort of approach

1192
00:58:36,570 --> 00:58:41,600
is that if your policy sees objects

1193
00:58:41,600 --> 00:58:43,810
that it's never seen before that are hard to describe

1194
00:58:43,810 --> 00:58:46,460
in language, then you--

1195
00:58:46,460 --> 00:58:49,180
obviously, that's something that it can more easily describe

1196
00:58:49,180 --> 00:58:52,180
in images because it can more easily generalize

1197
00:58:52,180 --> 00:58:53,700
across the objects that it's seeing

1198
00:58:53,700 --> 00:58:55,600
and generate pixels for those images

1199
00:58:55,600 --> 00:58:58,060
if it doesn't know what the semantic name for that object

1200
00:58:58,060 --> 00:58:59,780
is

1201
00:58:59,780 --> 00:59:00,920
Cool

1202
00:59:00,920 --> 00:59:02,420
And then for this sort of thing, you

1203
00:59:02,420 --> 00:59:05,010
can also do a comparison on like

1204
00:59:05,010 --> 00:59:08,660
is it helpful to include those unlabeled videos for training

1205
00:59:08,660 --> 00:59:10,740
the high-level policy

1206
00:59:10,740 --> 00:59:12,620
And if you only use robot data, you

1207
00:59:12,620 --> 00:59:14,340
get performance that looks like this

1208
00:59:14,340 --> 00:59:16,780
And if you add videos of humans for training

1209
00:59:16,780 --> 00:59:21,140
the high-level policy, you get an increase in performance

1210
00:59:21,140 --> 00:59:25,940
compared to just using the robot data to train the policy

1211
00:59:25,940 --> 00:59:30,300
Now, we talked earlier about how chain of thought

1212
00:59:30,300 --> 00:59:31,560
can replace hierarchy

1213
00:59:31,560 --> 00:59:33,860
And there's also approaches that have developed

1214
00:59:33,860 --> 00:59:36,260
chain of thoughts versions of this, where you can have

1215
00:59:36,260 --> 00:59:38,720
some high-level task and the current observation

1216
00:59:38,720 --> 00:59:42,290
and then actually output either subgoal images or subtasks

1217
00:59:42,290 --> 00:59:43,960
You can also have other representations

1218
00:59:43,960 --> 00:59:47,200
like bounding boxes or waypoints, and then

1219
00:59:47,200 --> 00:59:48,520
output the action

1220
00:59:48,520 --> 00:59:51,960
And there are some recent approaches

1221
00:59:51,960 --> 00:59:56,480
from the last one year, basically, quite recent

1222
00:59:56,480 --> 00:59:59,760
that do something like that

1223
00:59:59,760 --> 01:00:03,520
The other thing to note is that this is with imitation learning

1224
01:00:03,520 --> 01:00:05,040
And we'll talk about reinforcement

1225
01:00:05,040 --> 01:00:06,160
learning in a minute

1226
01:00:06,160 --> 01:00:09,920
But generally for these large-scale systems to date

1227
01:00:09,920 --> 01:00:12,640
for the most part, they use imitation learning more so

1228
01:00:12,640 --> 01:00:13,960
than reinforcement learning

1229
01:00:13,960 --> 01:00:17,600
And fine-tuning them with reinforcement learning

1230
01:00:17,600 --> 01:00:20,920
is, in some ways, an open research question

1231
01:00:20,920 --> 01:00:23,520
for like, what is the best recipe for accomplishing that

1232
01:00:23,520 --> 01:00:26,600
at large scales

1233
01:00:26,600 --> 01:00:28,600
It's also a pretty important research direction

1234
01:00:28,600 --> 01:00:31,570
because if we think about this slide right here

1235
01:00:31,570 --> 01:00:33,240
the kind of performance of these systems

1236
01:00:33,240 --> 01:00:36,260
is still not super, super reliable

1237
01:00:36,260 --> 01:00:38,760
I should mention, these are in pretty challenging situations

1238
01:00:38,760 --> 01:00:42,140
with held-out objects that the robot hasn't seen before

1239
01:00:42,140 --> 01:00:44,090
So it's kind of out of distribution compared

1240
01:00:44,090 --> 01:00:45,810
to what it saw during training

1241
01:00:45,810 --> 01:00:48,130
But if we had a way to train these with reinforcement

1242
01:00:48,130 --> 01:00:49,760
learning, then we should be able to get

1243
01:00:49,760 --> 01:00:52,170
these performances to be much higher compared

1244
01:00:52,170 --> 01:00:54,770
to using imitation learning

1245
01:00:54,770 --> 01:00:57,050
So the question is, like we talked about personalized

1246
01:00:57,050 --> 01:01:05,530
recommendation systems, could hierarchy be useful for that

1247
01:01:05,530 --> 01:01:08,150
It's not something that I've seen before

1248
01:01:08,150 --> 01:01:10,610
And generally when I've seen hierarchy

1249
01:01:10,610 --> 01:01:13,850
it's usually been for pretty longer-horizon problems

1250
01:01:13,850 --> 01:01:16,090
and tasks that are more complicated

1251
01:01:16,090 --> 01:01:18,010
In language modeling, it's been used

1252
01:01:18,010 --> 01:01:24,330
for math problems, essentially, and things

1253
01:01:24,330 --> 01:01:28,710
where you need to have multiple steps for completing something

1254
01:01:28,710 --> 01:01:29,210
Yeah

1255
01:01:29,210 --> 01:01:31,070
I could think about it

1256
01:01:31,070 --> 01:01:31,570
Yeah

1257
01:01:31,570 --> 01:01:34,370
So in this case, the high-level policy

1258
01:01:34,370 --> 01:01:37,190
is an image generative model

1259
01:01:37,190 --> 01:01:40,890
And what it does is it's going to take as input

1260
01:01:40,890 --> 01:01:44,590
It's trained to take as input, like an image

1261
01:01:44,590 --> 01:01:50,950
observation and some high-level goal, like goal high level

1262
01:01:50,950 --> 01:01:55,150
And then you have a neural network

1263
01:01:55,150 --> 01:01:57,590
And that neural network is going to be

1264
01:01:57,590 --> 01:02:02,320
trained to output Ot plus k

1265
01:02:02,320 --> 01:02:04,110
And so it's going to be trained to generate

1266
01:02:04,110 --> 01:02:08,670
an image of a future image

1267
01:02:08,670 --> 01:02:10,910
Yeah, trained to generate a future image

1268
01:02:10,910 --> 01:02:13,570
And you can get this as if you have videos

1269
01:02:13,570 --> 01:02:18,350
Then you don't need language annotations to get this image

1270
01:02:18,350 --> 01:02:21,550
In terms of the architecture, it's using a diffusion model

1271
01:02:21,550 --> 01:02:22,610
to generate these images

1272
01:02:22,610 --> 01:02:25,350
That's why you saw the noise moving

1273
01:02:25,350 --> 01:02:29,090
into the image in the direction of the goal

1274
01:02:29,090 --> 01:02:29,590
Yeah

1275
01:02:29,590 --> 01:02:34,270
So this particular goal might be on the horizon of like--

1276
01:02:34,270 --> 01:02:35,770
I don't know-- a minute

1277
01:02:35,770 --> 01:02:38,390
And this subgoal could be in the direction

1278
01:02:38,390 --> 01:02:39,470
or it could just be like--

1279
01:02:39,470 --> 01:02:42,740
I don't know-- 10 seconds in the future or something like that

1280
01:02:42,740 --> 01:02:43,240
Yeah

1281
01:02:43,240 --> 01:02:45,510
So in terms of using image-editing models

1282
01:02:45,510 --> 01:02:46,880
that general sort of pre-training

1283
01:02:46,880 --> 01:02:48,800
can be useful because it teaches the model how

1284
01:02:48,800 --> 01:02:50,480
to generate images, in general

1285
01:02:50,480 --> 01:02:52,420
And then the videos that are used

1286
01:02:52,420 --> 01:02:53,880
there are actually videos of people

1287
01:02:53,880 --> 01:02:55,900
doing different tasks that have--

1288
01:02:55,900 --> 01:02:58,240
like a single-language annotation for what the person

1289
01:02:58,240 --> 01:03:00,280
is doing in the whole video

1290
01:03:00,280 --> 01:03:03,040
And so those videos are especially

1291
01:03:03,040 --> 01:03:05,400
useful to include because they're pretty relevant

1292
01:03:05,400 --> 01:03:09,000
If instead you included random videos on YouTube

1293
01:03:09,000 --> 01:03:10,840
it's a little bit harder

1294
01:03:10,840 --> 01:03:12,690
But it might still teach it about--

1295
01:03:12,690 --> 01:03:15,240
if there's some future thing, what

1296
01:03:15,240 --> 01:03:17,600
are images that could happen in the future

1297
01:03:17,600 --> 01:03:21,020
And that is useful pre-training for this kind of task

1298
01:03:21,020 --> 01:03:21,520
Yeah

1299
01:03:21,520 --> 01:03:25,080
So you're asking like, what if the language isn't accurate

1300
01:03:25,080 --> 01:03:30,120
This is like a sketch instead of a real image

1301
01:03:30,120 --> 01:03:33,360
So good question

1302
01:03:33,360 --> 01:03:36,720
I guess, first, if you wanted to use sketches here

1303
01:03:36,720 --> 01:03:39,250
you just want to ideally train the low-level policy

1304
01:03:39,250 --> 01:03:40,790
to be able to use that sort of thing

1305
01:03:40,790 --> 01:03:42,170
And there are actually approaches

1306
01:03:42,170 --> 01:03:44,810
that have taken images, and you can convert them into a sketch

1307
01:03:44,810 --> 01:03:47,610
and train a low-level policy to do that

1308
01:03:47,610 --> 01:03:49,810
And you don't actually necessarily need

1309
01:03:49,810 --> 01:03:51,490
this to be a full image

1310
01:03:51,490 --> 01:03:53,290
These are just kind of, I should mention

1311
01:03:53,290 --> 01:03:54,830
two example representations

1312
01:03:54,830 --> 01:03:56,370
And language is actually particularly

1313
01:03:56,370 --> 01:03:58,920
popular as an intermediate representation

1314
01:03:58,920 --> 01:04:01,250
But there's lots of other representations you could use

1315
01:04:01,250 --> 01:04:04,110
including sketches, including--

1316
01:04:04,110 --> 01:04:06,650
I don't know-- like a trace of where you want the robot to go

1317
01:04:06,650 --> 01:04:09,330
or something like that

1318
01:04:09,330 --> 01:04:12,690
And then in cases where the language isn't accurate

1319
01:04:12,690 --> 01:04:14,970
if it is describing something that's different

1320
01:04:14,970 --> 01:04:18,770
then oftentimes the robot will then follow that different task

1321
01:04:18,770 --> 01:04:22,610
until it's been told to do something different

1322
01:04:22,610 --> 01:04:25,990
If the high-level policy is predicting language that is bad

1323
01:04:25,990 --> 01:04:29,450
then the system will suffer

1324
01:04:29,450 --> 01:04:33,370
There are some cases where the low-level policy might also

1325
01:04:33,370 --> 01:04:38,170
ignore the high-level policy

1326
01:04:38,170 --> 01:04:41,550
If you give it gibberish, it might just

1327
01:04:41,550 --> 01:04:44,860
have some sort of, in some ways, kind of default behavior

1328
01:04:44,860 --> 01:04:50,670
because it was trained to output actions

1329
01:04:50,670 --> 01:04:55,110
For different states, for example

1330
01:04:55,110 --> 01:05:00,630
if it's like holding the pasta right here

1331
01:05:00,630 --> 01:05:02,360
regardless of any sort of language input

1332
01:05:02,360 --> 01:05:04,490
chances are the next action is actually to pour it

1333
01:05:04,490 --> 01:05:06,090
And so if you don't give it a language instruction

1334
01:05:06,090 --> 01:05:08,170
or if you give it a noisy language instruction

1335
01:05:08,170 --> 01:05:09,710
it might still continue to pour it

1336
01:05:09,710 --> 01:05:11,610
just because it knows that for that state

1337
01:05:11,610 --> 01:05:14,150
typically, that's what you do next

1338
01:05:14,150 --> 01:05:16,670
Now let's talk briefly about hierarchical reinforcement

1339
01:05:16,670 --> 01:05:17,250
learning

1340
01:05:17,250 --> 01:05:19,620
So when I was bringing up examples of this

1341
01:05:19,620 --> 01:05:21,950
I think that the hierarchical imitation learning systems

1342
01:05:21,950 --> 01:05:28,710
are quite recent, and I don't have too many, I think

1343
01:05:28,710 --> 01:05:30,310
exciting examples, or examples that I

1344
01:05:30,310 --> 01:05:32,950
was excited about with regard to hierarchical reinforcement

1345
01:05:32,950 --> 01:05:35,050
learning

1346
01:05:35,050 --> 01:05:37,500
There are a few example systems that you can look at

1347
01:05:37,500 --> 01:05:39,880
and I think that generally getting these kinds of systems

1348
01:05:39,880 --> 01:05:41,800
to work really well, potentially on top

1349
01:05:41,800 --> 01:05:44,560
of the initialized hierarchical imitation learning systems--

1350
01:05:44,560 --> 01:05:46,600
something that would be, I think, really exciting

1351
01:05:46,600 --> 01:05:48,440
and important to explore

1352
01:05:48,440 --> 01:05:51,780
But generally, in terms of what has been done and what exists

1353
01:05:51,780 --> 01:05:53,320
you can do hierarchical reinforcement

1354
01:05:53,320 --> 01:05:57,200
learning with trying to reach different states

1355
01:05:57,200 --> 01:05:59,120
And this could be the relative target position

1356
01:05:59,120 --> 01:06:01,880
of some agents or some objects

1357
01:06:01,880 --> 01:06:07,000
And one cool work that does this is it

1358
01:06:07,000 --> 01:06:11,160
has this four-legged ant agent that you might have

1359
01:06:11,160 --> 01:06:13,840
worked with in homework one

1360
01:06:13,840 --> 01:06:16,260
And its goal is to get over here

1361
01:06:16,260 --> 01:06:17,680
And to get over there, it actually

1362
01:06:17,680 --> 01:06:20,200
needs to push this object to the right

1363
01:06:20,200 --> 01:06:23,840
and then move there as a result. And then

1364
01:06:23,840 --> 01:06:26,380
there's also this example, where it needs to get over here

1365
01:06:26,380 --> 01:06:27,760
If it tries to go directly there

1366
01:06:27,760 --> 01:06:29,680
it will fall in this crevasse

1367
01:06:29,680 --> 01:06:32,160
And instead, it needs to push and make

1368
01:06:32,160 --> 01:06:36,940
a little bridge for itself and walk over here

1369
01:06:36,940 --> 01:06:39,560
And these are pretty long-horizon problems

1370
01:06:39,560 --> 01:06:41,520
where if you just try to take actions greedily

1371
01:06:41,520 --> 01:06:44,060
you won't be able to get to the goal

1372
01:06:44,060 --> 01:06:47,900
And so the work trained a goal-conditioned policy

1373
01:06:47,900 --> 01:06:50,820
conditioned on goal states with a reward

1374
01:06:50,820 --> 01:06:52,340
of reaching those goal states

1375
01:06:52,340 --> 01:06:55,580
And then it trained a high-level policy to output goal states

1376
01:06:55,580 --> 01:06:58,930
And those goal states are the high-level actions

1377
01:06:58,930 --> 01:07:01,500
But it actually also used hindsight relabeling

1378
01:07:01,500 --> 01:07:07,700
for the high-level policy to actually

1379
01:07:07,700 --> 01:07:11,470
relabel high-level actions, or to relabel the goals

1380
01:07:11,470 --> 01:07:13,140
that the high-level policy should output

1381
01:07:13,140 --> 01:07:14,940
in certain circumstances

1382
01:07:14,940 --> 01:07:17,700
And it used an off-policy algorithm

1383
01:07:17,700 --> 01:07:24,580
to train this in order to allow it to work effectively

1384
01:07:24,580 --> 01:07:27,620
And then another work that does hierarchical reinforcement

1385
01:07:27,620 --> 01:07:29,360
learning uses language goals

1386
01:07:29,360 --> 01:07:30,640
So you could use goal states

1387
01:07:30,640 --> 01:07:33,420
You could also do language goals

1388
01:07:33,420 --> 01:07:35,850
This trains a language-conditioned policy

1389
01:07:35,850 --> 01:07:38,870
It uses language relabeling

1390
01:07:38,870 --> 01:07:40,630
So instead of goal relabeling, just

1391
01:07:40,630 --> 01:07:42,950
hindsight relabeling in language space

1392
01:07:42,950 --> 01:07:44,350
And then the high-level policy is

1393
01:07:44,350 --> 01:07:47,150
trained to output language, which is actually

1394
01:07:47,150 --> 01:07:51,270
a pretty difficult task

1395
01:07:51,270 --> 01:07:53,330
Again, this is a work that's not too recent

1396
01:07:53,330 --> 01:07:55,510
And so it's from 2019

1397
01:07:55,510 --> 01:07:57,910
And so the tasks are relatively simple

1398
01:07:57,910 --> 01:08:00,750
where it's trying to be able to arrange these different objects

1399
01:08:00,750 --> 01:08:03,290
in particular order, where you need to push the green object

1400
01:08:03,290 --> 01:08:08,710
push the red object, the blue object, and so forth

1401
01:08:08,710 --> 01:08:09,470
Cool

1402
01:08:09,470 --> 01:08:11,590
And then the last thing that I'll mention is that--

1403
01:08:11,590 --> 01:08:13,300
we've talked about these scenarios, where

1404
01:08:13,300 --> 01:08:16,229
you're supervising the low-level policy to reach some goals

1405
01:08:16,229 --> 01:08:18,790
and the high-level policy to output those goals

1406
01:08:18,790 --> 01:08:24,569
Now, there's, in some ways, a really cool research direction

1407
01:08:24,569 --> 01:08:26,990
of-- instead of actually supervising

1408
01:08:26,990 --> 01:08:31,710
it to do certain goals, can you actually discover

1409
01:08:31,710 --> 01:08:34,290
subtasks or discover skills that might

1410
01:08:34,290 --> 01:08:37,490
be useful without requiring supervision

1411
01:08:37,490 --> 01:08:40,609
on those low-level skills

1412
01:08:40,609 --> 01:08:45,090
And here's one example of a work that studies that, where it--

1413
01:08:45,090 --> 01:08:47,170
in this kind of simple half-cheetah agent

1414
01:08:47,170 --> 01:08:49,689
that you might have also seen in one of your homeworks

1415
01:08:49,689 --> 01:08:52,609
It's able to learn skills like backing up, walking

1416
01:08:52,609 --> 01:08:58,050
forward, doing I think a front flip, and so forth

1417
01:08:58,050 --> 01:09:00,229
And then in principle, if you discovered skills

1418
01:09:00,229 --> 01:09:01,689
without supervision, then you could

1419
01:09:01,689 --> 01:09:05,370
try to sequence those together to do harder tasks

1420
01:09:05,370 --> 01:09:07,529
But this is generally still an active research area

1421
01:09:07,529 --> 01:09:10,430
and something that's pretty challenging to scale up

1422
01:09:10,430 --> 01:09:14,729
especially in very large state spaces

1423
01:09:14,729 --> 01:09:15,930
Cool

1424
01:09:15,930 --> 01:09:16,649
That's it

1425
01:09:16,649 --> 01:09:18,770
And next time, we'll be talking about reinforcement

1426
01:09:18,770 --> 01:09:20,229
learning for robotics, specifically

1427
01:09:20,229 --> 01:09:23,569
learning with autonomy, and a guest

1428
01:09:23,569 --> 01:09:27,720
lecture from Ashish Kumar on the Tesla Optimus team

